window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2021-06-2", "source": "2021年06月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions:For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay titled“Is technology making people lazy?”.The statement given below is for your reference. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words .\n\nMany studies claim that computers distract people, make them lazy thinkers and even lower their work efficiency.", "sample": "Is technology making people lazy? My answer is a qualified yes: while technology itself is neutral, the way\nmost of us use it does breed laziness of both mind and body.\nTo begin with, technology encourages mental laziness. When a search engine can answer any question in\nseconds and a navigation app can guide every turn, few people bother to think, memorize or reason on their\nown. Gradually, we outsource our brains and lose the habit of deep thinking. Physical laziness follows the\nsame logic: with delivery apps and online services, many people rarely walk, cook or move, so their bodies\ngrow as idle as their minds.\nAdmittedly, technology can also make us more efficient. Yet the tool is not to blame; our passive dependence\nis. Therefore, the real question is not whether technology makes us lazy, but whether we control it or let it\ncontrol us. Used wisely, it frees us; used blindly, it enslaves us."}, "translation": {"prompt": "普洱(Pur'er)茶深受中国人喜爱。最好的普洱茶产自云南的西双版纳(Xishuangbanna)，那里的气候和环境为普洱茶树的生长提供了最佳条件。普洱茶颜色较深，味道与其他许多茶截然不同。普洱茶泡(brew)的时间越长越有味道。许多爱喝茶的人尤其喜欢其独特的香味和口感。普洱茶含有多种有益健康的元素，常饮普洱茶有助于保护心脏和血管，还有减肥、消除疲劳和促进消化的功效。", "sample": "Pu'er tea (also known as Pur'er tea) is deeply loved by the Chinese people. The best Pu'er tea comes from Xishuangbanna in Yunnan Province, where the climate and environment provide the ideal conditions for the growth of Pu'er tea trees. It has a darker color and a taste that is quite different from that of many other teas. The longer Pu'er tea is brewed, the more flavorful it becomes. Moreover, many tea lovers especially enjoy its unique aroma and taste. Pu'er tea contains many elements beneficial to health. Drinking it regularly helps protect the heart and blood vessels, and also helps lose weight, relieve fatigue and promote digestion."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2021-06-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Social isolation poses more health risks than obesity or smoking 15 cigarettes a day, according to research published by Brigham Young University. The  __26__  is that loneliness is a huge, if silent, risk factor.\nLoneliness affects physical health in two ways. First, it produces stress hormones that can lead to many health problems. Second, people who live alone are less likely to go to doctor  __27__  , to exercise or to eat a healthy diet.\nPublic health experts in many countries are  __28__  how to address widespread loneliness in our society. Last year Britain even appointed a minister for loneliness. \"Loneliness  __29__  almost every one of us at some point,\" its minister for loneliness Baroness Barran said. \"It can lead to very serious health  __30__  for individuals who become isolated and disconnected.\"\nBarran started a \"Let's Talk Loneliness\" campaign that  __31__  difficult conversations across Britain. He is now supporting \"  __32__  benches,\" which are public seating areas where people are encouraged to go and chat with one another. The minister is also  __33__  to stop public transportation from being cut in ways that leave people isolated.\nMore than one-fifth of adults in both the United States and Britain said in a 2018  __34__  that they often or always feel lonely. More than half of American adults are unmarried, and researchers have found that even among those who are married, 30% of relationships are  __35__  strained. A quarter of Americans now live alone, and as the song says, one is the loneliest number.", "matchIntro": "What happens when a language has no words for numbers?", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Numbers do not exist in all cultures. There are numberless hunter-gatherers in Amazonia, living along branches of the world's largest river tree. Instead of using words for precise quantities, these people rely exclusively on terms similar to \"a few\" or \"some.\" In contrast, our own lives are governed by numbers. As you read this, you are likely aware of what time it is, how old you are, your checking account balance, your weight and so on. The exact numbers we think with impact everything in our lives."}, {"label": "B", "text": "But, in a historical sense, number-conscious people like us are the unusual ones. For the bulk of our species' approximately 200,000-year lifespan, we had no means of precisely representing quantities. What's more, the 7,000 or so languages that exist today vary dramatically in how they utilize numbers."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Speakers of anumeric, or numberless, languages offer a window into how the invention of numbers reshaped the human experience. Cultures without numbers, or with only one or two precise numbers, include the Munduruku and Piraha in Amazonia. Researchers have also studied some adults in Nicaragua who were never taught number words. Without numbers, healthy human adults struggle to precisely distinguish and recall quantities as low as four. In an experiment, a researcher will place nuts into a can one at a time and then remove them one by one. The person watching is asked to signal when all the nuts have been removed. Responses suggest that anumeric people have some trouble keeping track of how many nuts remain in the can, even if there are only four or five in total."}, {"label": "D", "text": "This and many other experiments have led to a simple conclusion: When people do not have number words, they struggle to make quantitative distinctions that probably seem natural to someone like you or me. While only a small portion of the world's languages are anumeric or nearly anumeric, they demonstrate that number words are not a human universal."}, {"label": "E", "text": "It is worth stressing that these anumeric people are cognitively(在认知方面) normal, well-adapted to the surroundings they have dominated for centuries. As a child, I spent some time living with anumeric people, the Piraha who live along the banks of the black Maici River. Like other outsiders, I was continually impressed by their superior understanding of the ecology we shared. Yet numberless people struggle with tasks that require precise discrimination between quantities. Perhaps this should be unsurprising. After all, without counting, how can someone tell whether there are, say, seven or eight coconuts(椰子) in a tree? Such seemingly straightforward distinctions become blurry through numberless eyes."}, {"label": "F", "text": "This conclusion is echoed by work with anumeric children in industrialized societies. Prior to being spoon-fed number words, children can only approximately discriminate quantities beyond three. We must be handed the cognitive tools of numbers before we can consistently and easily recognize higher quantities. In fact, acquiring the exact meaning of number words is a painstaking process that takes children years. Initially, kids learn numbers much like they learn letters. They recognize that numbers are organized sequentially, but have little awareness of what each individual number means. With time, they start to understand that a given number represents a quantity greater by one than the number coming before it. This \"successor principle\" is part of the foundation of our numerical(数字的) cognition, but requires extensive practice to understand."}, {"label": "G", "text": "None of us, then, is really a \"numbers person.\" We are not born to handle quantitative distinctions skillfully. In the absence of the cultural traditions that fill our lives with numbers from infancy, we would all struggle with even basic quantitative distinctions. Number words and their written forms transform our quantitative reasoning as they are introduced into our cognitive experience by our parents, peers and school teachers. The process seems so normal that we sometimes think of it as a natural part of growing up, but it is not. Human brains come equipped with certain quantitative instincts that are refined with age, but these instincts are very limited."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Compared with other mammals, our numerical instincts are not as remarkable as many assume. We even share some basic instinctual quantitative reasoning with distant non-mammalian relatives like birds. Indeed, work with some other species suggests they too can refine their quantitative thought if they are introduced to the cognitive power tools we call numbers."}, {"label": "I", "text": "So, how did we ever invent \"unnatural\" numbers in the first place? The answer is, literally, at your fingertips. The bulk of the world's languages use base-10, base-20 or base-5 number systems. That is, these smaller numbers are the basis of larger numbers. English is a base-10 or decimal(十进制的) language, as evidenced by words like 14(\"four\" + \"10\") and 31 (\"three\" x \"10\" + \"one\"). We speak a decimal language because an ancestral tongue, proto-Indo-European, was decimally based. Proto-Indo-European was decimally oriented because, as in so many cultures, our ancestors' hands served as the gateway to the realization that \"five fingers on one hand is the same as five fingers on the other.\" Such momentary thoughts were represented in words and passed down across generations. This is why the word \"five\" in many languages is derived from the word for \"hand.\" Most number systems, then, are the by-product of two key factors: the human capacity for language and our inclination for focusing on our hands and fingers. This manual fixation—an indirect by-product of walking upright on two legs—has helped yield numbers in most cultures, but not all."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Cultures without numbers also offer insight into the cognitive influence of particular numeric traditions. Consider what time it is. Your day is ruled by minutes and seconds, but these concepts are not real in any physical sense and are nonexistent to numberless people. Minutes and seconds are the verbal and written representations of an uncommon base-60 number system used in ancient Mesopotamia. They reside in our minds, numerical artifacts(人工制品) that not all humans inherit conceptually."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Research on the language of numbers shows, more and more, that one of our species' key characteristics is tremendous linguistic(语言的) and cognitive diversity. If we are to truly understand how much our cognitive lives differ cross-culturally, we must continually explore the depths of our species' linguistic diversity."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Sugar shocked. That describes the reaction of many Americans this week following revelations that, 50 years ago, the sugar industry paid Harvard scientists for research that shifted the focus away from sugar's role in heart disease—and put the spotlight(注意的中心) squarely on dietary fat.\nWhat might surprise consumers is just how many present-day nutrition studies are still funded by the food industry. Nutrition scholar Marion Nestle of New York University spent a year informally tracking industry-funded studies on food. \"Roughly 90% of nearly 170 studies favored the sponsor's interest,\" Nestle tells us. Other systematic reviews support her conclusions.\nFor instance, studies funded by Welch Foods—the brand behind Welch's 100% Grape Juice—found that drinking Concord grape juice daily may boost brain function. Another, funded by Quaker Oats, concluded, as a Daily Mail story put it, that \"hot oatmeal(燕麦粥) breakfast keeps you full for longer.\"\nLast year, The New York Times revealed how Coca-Cola was funding well-known scientists and organizations promoting a message that, in the battle against weight gain, people should pay more attention to exercise and less to what they eat and drink. Coca-Cola also released data detailing its funding of several medical institutions and associations between 2010 and 2015.\n\"It's certainly a problem that so much research in nutrition and health is funded by industry,\" says Bonnie Liebman, director of nutrition at the Center for Science in the Public Interest. \"When the food industry pays for research, it often gets what it pays for.\" And what it pays for is often a pro-industry finding.\nGiven this environment, consumers should be skeptical(怀疑的) when reading the latest finding in nutrition science and ignore the latest study that pops up on your news feed. \"Rely on health experts who've reviewed all the evidence,\" Liebman says, pointing to the official government Dietary Guidelines, which are based on reviews of hundreds of studies.\n\"And that expert advice remains pretty simple,\" says Nestle. \"We know what healthy diets are—lots of vegetables, not too much junk food, balanced calories. Everything else is really difficult to do experimentally.\"", "47": "Sugar shocked. That describes the reaction of many Americans this week following revelations that, 50 years ago, the sugar industry paid Harvard scientists for research that shifted the focus away from sugar's role in heart disease—and put the spotlight(注意的中心) squarely on dietary fat.\nWhat might surprise consumers is just how many present-day nutrition studies are still funded by the food industry. Nutrition scholar Marion Nestle of New York University spent a year informally tracking industry-funded studies on food. \"Roughly 90% of nearly 170 studies favored the sponsor's interest,\" Nestle tells us. Other systematic reviews support her conclusions.\nFor instance, studies funded by Welch Foods—the brand behind Welch's 100% Grape Juice—found that drinking Concord grape juice daily may boost brain function. Another, funded by Quaker Oats, concluded, as a Daily Mail story put it, that \"hot oatmeal(燕麦粥) breakfast keeps you full for longer.\"\nLast year, The New York Times revealed how Coca-Cola was funding well-known scientists and organizations promoting a message that, in the battle against weight gain, people should pay more attention to exercise and less to what they eat and drink. Coca-Cola also released data detailing its funding of several medical institutions and associations between 2010 and 2015.\n\"It's certainly a problem that so much research in nutrition and health is funded by industry,\" says Bonnie Liebman, director of nutrition at the Center for Science in the Public Interest. \"When the food industry pays for research, it often gets what it pays for.\" And what it pays for is often a pro-industry finding.\nGiven this environment, consumers should be skeptical(怀疑的) when reading the latest finding in nutrition science and ignore the latest study that pops up on your news feed. \"Rely on health experts who've reviewed all the evidence,\" Liebman says, pointing to the official government Dietary Guidelines, which are based on reviews of hundreds of studies.\n\"And that expert advice remains pretty simple,\" says Nestle. \"We know what healthy diets are—lots of vegetables, not too much junk food, balanced calories. Everything else is really difficult to do experimentally.\"", "48": "Sugar shocked. That describes the reaction of many Americans this week following revelations that, 50 years ago, the sugar industry paid Harvard scientists for research that shifted the focus away from sugar's role in heart disease—and put the spotlight(注意的中心) squarely on dietary fat.\nWhat might surprise consumers is just how many present-day nutrition studies are still funded by the food industry. Nutrition scholar Marion Nestle of New York University spent a year informally tracking industry-funded studies on food. \"Roughly 90% of nearly 170 studies favored the sponsor's interest,\" Nestle tells us. Other systematic reviews support her conclusions.\nFor instance, studies funded by Welch Foods—the brand behind Welch's 100% Grape Juice—found that drinking Concord grape juice daily may boost brain function. Another, funded by Quaker Oats, concluded, as a Daily Mail story put it, that \"hot oatmeal(燕麦粥) breakfast keeps you full for longer.\"\nLast year, The New York Times revealed how Coca-Cola was funding well-known scientists and organizations promoting a message that, in the battle against weight gain, people should pay more attention to exercise and less to what they eat and drink. Coca-Cola also released data detailing its funding of several medical institutions and associations between 2010 and 2015.\n\"It's certainly a problem that so much research in nutrition and health is funded by industry,\" says Bonnie Liebman, director of nutrition at the Center for Science in the Public Interest. \"When the food industry pays for research, it often gets what it pays for.\" And what it pays for is often a pro-industry finding.\nGiven this environment, consumers should be skeptical(怀疑的) when reading the latest finding in nutrition science and ignore the latest study that pops up on your news feed. \"Rely on health experts who've reviewed all the evidence,\" Liebman says, pointing to the official government Dietary Guidelines, which are based on reviews of hundreds of studies.\n\"And that expert advice remains pretty simple,\" says Nestle. \"We know what healthy diets are—lots of vegetables, not too much junk food, balanced calories. Everything else is really difficult to do experimentally.\"", "49": "Sugar shocked. That describes the reaction of many Americans this week following revelations that, 50 years ago, the sugar industry paid Harvard scientists for research that shifted the focus away from sugar's role in heart disease—and put the spotlight(注意的中心) squarely on dietary fat.\nWhat might surprise consumers is just how many present-day nutrition studies are still funded by the food industry. Nutrition scholar Marion Nestle of New York University spent a year informally tracking industry-funded studies on food. \"Roughly 90% of nearly 170 studies favored the sponsor's interest,\" Nestle tells us. Other systematic reviews support her conclusions.\nFor instance, studies funded by Welch Foods—the brand behind Welch's 100% Grape Juice—found that drinking Concord grape juice daily may boost brain function. Another, funded by Quaker Oats, concluded, as a Daily Mail story put it, that \"hot oatmeal(燕麦粥) breakfast keeps you full for longer.\"\nLast year, The New York Times revealed how Coca-Cola was funding well-known scientists and organizations promoting a message that, in the battle against weight gain, people should pay more attention to exercise and less to what they eat and drink. Coca-Cola also released data detailing its funding of several medical institutions and associations between 2010 and 2015.\n\"It's certainly a problem that so much research in nutrition and health is funded by industry,\" says Bonnie Liebman, director of nutrition at the Center for Science in the Public Interest. \"When the food industry pays for research, it often gets what it pays for.\" And what it pays for is often a pro-industry finding.\nGiven this environment, consumers should be skeptical(怀疑的) when reading the latest finding in nutrition science and ignore the latest study that pops up on your news feed. \"Rely on health experts who've reviewed all the evidence,\" Liebman says, pointing to the official government Dietary Guidelines, which are based on reviews of hundreds of studies.\n\"And that expert advice remains pretty simple,\" says Nestle. \"We know what healthy diets are—lots of vegetables, not too much junk food, balanced calories. Everything else is really difficult to do experimentally.\"", "50": "Sugar shocked. That describes the reaction of many Americans this week following revelations that, 50 years ago, the sugar industry paid Harvard scientists for research that shifted the focus away from sugar's role in heart disease—and put the spotlight(注意的中心) squarely on dietary fat.\nWhat might surprise consumers is just how many present-day nutrition studies are still funded by the food industry. Nutrition scholar Marion Nestle of New York University spent a year informally tracking industry-funded studies on food. \"Roughly 90% of nearly 170 studies favored the sponsor's interest,\" Nestle tells us. Other systematic reviews support her conclusions.\nFor instance, studies funded by Welch Foods—the brand behind Welch's 100% Grape Juice—found that drinking Concord grape juice daily may boost brain function. Another, funded by Quaker Oats, concluded, as a Daily Mail story put it, that \"hot oatmeal(燕麦粥) breakfast keeps you full for longer.\"\nLast year, The New York Times revealed how Coca-Cola was funding well-known scientists and organizations promoting a message that, in the battle against weight gain, people should pay more attention to exercise and less to what they eat and drink. Coca-Cola also released data detailing its funding of several medical institutions and associations between 2010 and 2015.\n\"It's certainly a problem that so much research in nutrition and health is funded by industry,\" says Bonnie Liebman, director of nutrition at the Center for Science in the Public Interest. \"When the food industry pays for research, it often gets what it pays for.\" And what it pays for is often a pro-industry finding.\nGiven this environment, consumers should be skeptical(怀疑的) when reading the latest finding in nutrition science and ignore the latest study that pops up on your news feed. \"Rely on health experts who've reviewed all the evidence,\" Liebman says, pointing to the official government Dietary Guidelines, which are based on reviews of hundreds of studies.\n\"And that expert advice remains pretty simple,\" says Nestle. \"We know what healthy diets are—lots of vegetables, not too much junk food, balanced calories. Everything else is really difficult to do experimentally.\"", "51": "Success was once defined as being able to stay at a company for a long time and move up the corporate ladder. The goal was to reach the top, accumulate wealth and retire to a life of ease. My father is a successful senior executive. In 35 years, he worked for only three companies.\nWhen I started my career, things were already different. If you weren't changing companies every three or four years, you simply weren't getting ahead in your career. But back then, if you were a consultant or freelancer(自由职业者), people would wonder what was wrong with you. They would assume you had problems getting a job.\nToday, consulting or freelancing for five businesses at the same time is a badge of honor. It shows how valuable an individual is. Many companies now look to these \"ultimate professionals\" to solve problems their full-time teams can't. Or they save money by hiring \"top-tier(顶尖的) experts\" only for particular projects.\nWorking at home or in cafes, starting businesses of their own, and even launching business ventures that eventually may fail, all indicate \"initiative,\" \"creativity,\" and \"adaptability,\" which are desirable qualities in today's workplace. Most important, there is a growing recognition that people who balance work and play, and who work at what they are passionate about, are more focused and productive, delivering greater value to their clients.\nWho are these people? They are artists, writers, programmers, providers of office services and career advice. What's needed now is a marketplace platform specifically designed to bring freelancers and clients together. Such platforms then become a place to feature the most experienced, professional, and creative talent. This is where they conduct business, where a sense of community reinforces the culture and values of the gig economy(零工经济), and where success is rewarded with good reviews that encourage more business.\nSlowly but surely, these platforms create a bridge between traditional enterprises and this emerging economy. Perhaps more important, as the global economy continues to be disrupted by technology and other massive change, the gig economy will itself become an engine of economic and social transformation.", "52": "Success was once defined as being able to stay at a company for a long time and move up the corporate ladder. The goal was to reach the top, accumulate wealth and retire to a life of ease. My father is a successful senior executive. In 35 years, he worked for only three companies.\nWhen I started my career, things were already different. If you weren't changing companies every three or four years, you simply weren't getting ahead in your career. But back then, if you were a consultant or freelancer(自由职业者), people would wonder what was wrong with you. They would assume you had problems getting a job.\nToday, consulting or freelancing for five businesses at the same time is a badge of honor. It shows how valuable an individual is. Many companies now look to these \"ultimate professionals\" to solve problems their full-time teams can't. Or they save money by hiring \"top-tier(顶尖的) experts\" only for particular projects.\nWorking at home or in cafes, starting businesses of their own, and even launching business ventures that eventually may fail, all indicate \"initiative,\" \"creativity,\" and \"adaptability,\" which are desirable qualities in today's workplace. Most important, there is a growing recognition that people who balance work and play, and who work at what they are passionate about, are more focused and productive, delivering greater value to their clients.\nWho are these people? They are artists, writers, programmers, providers of office services and career advice. What's needed now is a marketplace platform specifically designed to bring freelancers and clients together. Such platforms then become a place to feature the most experienced, professional, and creative talent. This is where they conduct business, where a sense of community reinforces the culture and values of the gig economy(零工经济), and where success is rewarded with good reviews that encourage more business.\nSlowly but surely, these platforms create a bridge between traditional enterprises and this emerging economy. Perhaps more important, as the global economy continues to be disrupted by technology and other massive change, the gig economy will itself become an engine of economic and social transformation.", "53": "Success was once defined as being able to stay at a company for a long time and move up the corporate ladder. The goal was to reach the top, accumulate wealth and retire to a life of ease. My father is a successful senior executive. In 35 years, he worked for only three companies.\nWhen I started my career, things were already different. If you weren't changing companies every three or four years, you simply weren't getting ahead in your career. But back then, if you were a consultant or freelancer(自由职业者), people would wonder what was wrong with you. They would assume you had problems getting a job.\nToday, consulting or freelancing for five businesses at the same time is a badge of honor. It shows how valuable an individual is. Many companies now look to these \"ultimate professionals\" to solve problems their full-time teams can't. Or they save money by hiring \"top-tier(顶尖的) experts\" only for particular projects.\nWorking at home or in cafes, starting businesses of their own, and even launching business ventures that eventually may fail, all indicate \"initiative,\" \"creativity,\" and \"adaptability,\" which are desirable qualities in today's workplace. Most important, there is a growing recognition that people who balance work and play, and who work at what they are passionate about, are more focused and productive, delivering greater value to their clients.\nWho are these people? They are artists, writers, programmers, providers of office services and career advice. What's needed now is a marketplace platform specifically designed to bring freelancers and clients together. Such platforms then become a place to feature the most experienced, professional, and creative talent. This is where they conduct business, where a sense of community reinforces the culture and values of the gig economy(零工经济), and where success is rewarded with good reviews that encourage more business.\nSlowly but surely, these platforms create a bridge between traditional enterprises and this emerging economy. Perhaps more important, as the global economy continues to be disrupted by technology and other massive change, the gig economy will itself become an engine of economic and social transformation.", "54": "Success was once defined as being able to stay at a company for a long time and move up the corporate ladder. The goal was to reach the top, accumulate wealth and retire to a life of ease. My father is a successful senior executive. In 35 years, he worked for only three companies.\nWhen I started my career, things were already different. If you weren't changing companies every three or four years, you simply weren't getting ahead in your career. But back then, if you were a consultant or freelancer(自由职业者), people would wonder what was wrong with you. They would assume you had problems getting a job.\nToday, consulting or freelancing for five businesses at the same time is a badge of honor. It shows how valuable an individual is. Many companies now look to these \"ultimate professionals\" to solve problems their full-time teams can't. Or they save money by hiring \"top-tier(顶尖的) experts\" only for particular projects.\nWorking at home or in cafes, starting businesses of their own, and even launching business ventures that eventually may fail, all indicate \"initiative,\" \"creativity,\" and \"adaptability,\" which are desirable qualities in today's workplace. Most important, there is a growing recognition that people who balance work and play, and who work at what they are passionate about, are more focused and productive, delivering greater value to their clients.\nWho are these people? They are artists, writers, programmers, providers of office services and career advice. What's needed now is a marketplace platform specifically designed to bring freelancers and clients together. Such platforms then become a place to feature the most experienced, professional, and creative talent. This is where they conduct business, where a sense of community reinforces the culture and values of the gig economy(零工经济), and where success is rewarded with good reviews that encourage more business.\nSlowly but surely, these platforms create a bridge between traditional enterprises and this emerging economy. Perhaps more important, as the global economy continues to be disrupted by technology and other massive change, the gig economy will itself become an engine of economic and social transformation.", "55": "Success was once defined as being able to stay at a company for a long time and move up the corporate ladder. The goal was to reach the top, accumulate wealth and retire to a life of ease. My father is a successful senior executive. In 35 years, he worked for only three companies.\nWhen I started my career, things were already different. If you weren't changing companies every three or four years, you simply weren't getting ahead in your career. But back then, if you were a consultant or freelancer(自由职业者), people would wonder what was wrong with you. They would assume you had problems getting a job.\nToday, consulting or freelancing for five businesses at the same time is a badge of honor. It shows how valuable an individual is. Many companies now look to these \"ultimate professionals\" to solve problems their full-time teams can't. Or they save money by hiring \"top-tier(顶尖的) experts\" only for particular projects.\nWorking at home or in cafes, starting businesses of their own, and even launching business ventures that eventually may fail, all indicate \"initiative,\" \"creativity,\" and \"adaptability,\" which are desirable qualities in today's workplace. Most important, there is a growing recognition that people who balance work and play, and who work at what they are passionate about, are more focused and productive, delivering greater value to their clients.\nWho are these people? They are artists, writers, programmers, providers of office services and career advice. What's needed now is a marketplace platform specifically designed to bring freelancers and clients together. Such platforms then become a place to feature the most experienced, professional, and creative talent. This is where they conduct business, where a sense of community reinforces the culture and values of the gig economy(零工经济), and where success is rewarded with good reviews that encourage more business.\nSlowly but surely, these platforms create a bridge between traditional enterprises and this emerging economy. Perhaps more important, as the global economy continues to be disrupted by technology and other massive change, the gig economy will itself become an engine of economic and social transformation."}, "questions": [{"id": "2021-06-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What did the old age pensioner plan to do?", "options": ["A) See the Pope.", "B) Go to Newcastle.", "C) Travel to Germany.", "D) Tour an Italian city."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） See the Pope.\n【定位】The old age pensioner was on a mission to see the Pope, who is head of the Catholic Church,\nand decided to make his way there by car.\n这位领养老金的老人肩负着去见教皇（天主教会的领袖）的使命，并决定开车前往那里。\n【信号】表目的的 on a mission to + decided to。答案信号是表 \"目的 / 决意\" 的措辞：on a\nmission to see the Pope（肩负见教皇的使命）和 decided to make his way there（决定\n前往）。听力里 \"on a mission to / decided to / plan to\" 后面跟的就是主人公的目标，一\n听到就锁定，这正是 plan 类题的落点。\n【替换】on a mission to see the Pope ↔ See the Pope.\n去修饰语后核心动作不变\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音里 trying to get from Newcastle 确实念到了 Newcastle，但那是他的 \"出发地\"，不\n是要 \"go to\" 的目的地。选项 Go to Newcastle. 盗用这个原词，把起点说成终点，听到\nNewcastle 就选必中招。\n借用原文：trying to get from Newcastle, Great Britain, to Rome, Italy\nC) 答非所问\nGermany 是他 \"误打误撞最后到达\" 的地方（ended up in Germany by accident），并非\n他 \"打算\" 去的地方。选项 Travel to Germany. 把 \"意外结果\" 安到 \"计划\" 头上，正是本题\n问 plan 时最阴的干扰。\n借用原文：ended up in Germany by accident\nD) 偷换概念\n罗马（Italian city）虽是他的真正目的地，但 Tour an Italian city. 把目的说成 \"游览一座意\n大利城市\"，与录音 on a mission to see the Pope（去见教皇）这一明确目的不符——他\n不是去观光，是去见教皇。选项借意大利城市这层背景来诱选。\n借用原文：on a mission to see the Pope", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What finally happened to the old man?", "options": ["A) He was taken to hospital in an ambulance.", "B) His car hit a sign and was badly damaged.", "C) His GPS system went out of order.", "D) He ended up in the wrong place."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） He ended up in the wrong place.\n【定位】An 81-year-old man ended up in Germany by accident after trying to get from Newcastle,\nGreat Britain, to Rome, Italy.\n一位 81 岁的老人在试图从英国纽卡斯尔前往意大利罗马的途中，意外地落到了德国。\n【信号】结果词 ended up + by accident。答案信号是表 \"最终结果\" 的措辞 ended up in\nGermany by accident：ended up（最终落到…）+ by accident（出于意外）直接点出\n\"跑错了地方\" 这个结局。问 finally happened 的题，就抓 ended up / finally / in the end\n这类收束词。\n【替换】ended up in Germany by accident ↔ He ended up in the wrong place.\n具体地名概括成 wrong place\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音 an ambulance was called to check on the pensioner 是 \"叫了救护车来查看\"，并\n没说把他送进医院。选项 He was taken to hospital in an ambulance. 盗用 ambulance\n这个原词，再加码成 \"被送往医院\"，听到 ambulance 就选必错。\n借用原文：an ambulance was called to check on the pensioner\nB) 与原文相反\n车确实 hit a sign（撞了路牌），但录音明说 did not appear to be seriously damaged\n（看起来没严重损坏）。选项 His car hit a sign and was badly damaged. 前半句盗用原\n词为真、后半 badly damaged 与原文相反，是典型 \"半真半假\" 陷阱。\n借用原文：did not appear to be seriously damaged\nC) 张冠李戴\n录音里 GPS 一直正常工作（his GPS system told him he had arrived），错的是老人把目\n的地 \"Rom\" 输错了，不是 GPS 坏了。选项 His GPS system went out of order. 盗用 GPS\n这个高频原词，硬安一个 \"故障\" 给它，把人为输错说成机器失灵。\n借用原文：his GPS system told him he had arrived\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 Glasgow has pledged to become the first carbon neutral city in the UK. The city's council and\nScottishPower have announced a range of strategies in an attempt to reduce carbon emissions ahead of\nthe new national target of 2045.\nGlasgow承诺成为英国首个碳中和城市。该市议会和ScottishPower宣布了一系列战略，试图在2045年这\n一新的国家目标之前减少碳排放。\n2 First Minister Nicola Sturgeon welcomed the pledge, and said: \"Today's announcement between\nScottishPower and Glasgow City Council—to make Glasgow the UK's first net-zero city—is a very\nwelcome step. Reaching our goals will need exactly this kind of partnership approach—with government,\nbusiness, local authorities and citizens all playing their part.\"\n首席大臣Nicola Sturgeon对这一承诺表示欢迎，并说：\"今天ScottishPower与Glasgow市议会之间的宣\n布——使Glasgow成为英国首个净零排放城市——是非常值得欢迎的一步。实现我们的目标需要正是这\n种伙伴关系方式——政府、企业、地方当局和公民都发挥各自的作用。\"\n3 Speaking ahead of the All Energy Conference being held in Glasgow, ScottishPower chief executive\nKeith Anderson said: \"We have a large supply of renewable energy on our doorstep and one of the only\ntwo low emission zones in action across the UK. Now, we need to invest in the technologies and\nprogrammes that transform the rest of Glasgow's economy and make us net zero before anyone else.\"\n在Glasgow举行的全能源大会之前发言时,ScottishPower首席执行官Keith Anderson说：\"我们家门口有\n大量的可再生能源供应，并且拥有全英国仅有的两个低排放区之一。现在，我们需要投资于技术和项目，\n以转变Glasgow经济的其他部分，使我们比其他任何人都更早实现净零排放。\"", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What do we learn from the news report?", "options": ["A) Scotland will reach the national target in carbon emissions reduction ahead of schedule.", "B) Glasgow City Council has made a deal with ScottishPower on carbon emissions.", "C) Glasgow has pledged to take the lead in reducing carbon emissions in the UK.", "D) First Minister Nicola Sturgeon urged ScottishPower to reduce carbon emissions."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （主旨题） Glasgow has pledged to take the lead in reducing carbon emissions in the UK.\n【定位】Glasgow has pledged to become the first carbon neutral city in the UK.\n格拉斯哥已承诺要成为英国第一个碳中和城市。\n【信号】新闻首句即点题。信号是「新闻第一句」：英文短消息几乎都把最重要的事放在开头第一\n句。Glasgow has pledged to become the first carbon neutral city in the UK. 正是全篇\n核心事件，主旨题答案就藏在这句里。\n【替换】pledged to become the first carbon neutral city in the UK ↔\npledged to take the lead in reducing carbon emissions in the UK\n「第一个碳中和」=「带头减排」\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 盗用了录音 the new national target of 2045 里的 national target 一词，编出「苏格兰\n将提前达成全国减排目标」。但录音只说格拉斯哥要「赶在 2045 全国目标之前（ahead\nof）」实现零碳，是这一座城市立下承诺，并非「苏格兰整体将提前达标」，属听到 target\n就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：the new national target of 2045\nB) 答非所问\nB 盗用了录音多次出现的 ScottishPower 和 Glasgow City Council，说成「市议会与\nScottishPower 就碳排放达成一桩交易」。录音里二者确有合作（announced a range of\nstrategies），但新闻主旨是「格拉斯哥承诺成为英国首个零碳城市」这件大事，B 把主旨\n缩成一个次要的「达成 deal」细节，以偏概全。\n借用原文：The city's council and ScottishPower have announced a range of strategies\nD) 张冠李戴\nD 盗用了录音里的 First Minister Nicola Sturgeon 和 ScottishPower 两个专名，编成\n「Sturgeon 敦促 ScottishPower 减排」。但录音里 Sturgeon 是 welcomed the pledge\n（对承诺表示欢迎），并没有「敦促」ScottishPower，方向与事实都不符，是把人名拼凑\n成假信息的张冠李戴。\n借用原文：First Minister Nicola Sturgeon welcomed the pledge", "points": ["听力·长对话", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What did ScottishPower's chief executive say ahead of the All Energy Conference?", "options": ["A) Glasgow needs to invest in new technologies to reach its goal.", "B) Glasgow is going to explore new sources of renewable energy.", "C) Stricter regulation is needed in transforming Glasgow's economy.", "D) It's necessary to create more low-emission zones as soon as possible."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Glasgow needs to invest in new technologies to reach its goal.\n【定位】Now, we need to invest in the technologies and programmes that transform the rest of\nGlasgow's economy and make us net zero before anyone else.\n现在，我们需要投资于那些能够改造格拉斯哥其余经济、让我们比任何人都更早实现净零排\n放的技术与项目。\n【信号】主张句 we need to… 是落点。信号是说话人亲口的主张句 we need to invest in the\ntechnologies and programmes：题问「他说了什么」，答案就在他原话里 we need to\n（我们需要）这个表态结构后面，这是观点的直接落点。\n【替换】we need to invest in the technologies and programmes ↔\nGlasgow needs to invest in new technologies to reach its goal\n「投资于技术」原意保留，仅换措辞\nmake us net zero before anyone else ↔ to reach its goal\n「率先实现净零」概括成「达成目标」\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 盗用了录音 a large supply of renewable energy 里的 renewable energy。但说话人说\n的是「我们已经拥有大量可再生能源」，是现状陈述，并非主张「去探索新的可再生能源来\n源（explore new sources）」。听到 renewable energy 就选 B 正中圈套。\n借用原文：We have a large supply of renewable energy on our doorstep\nC) 未提及\nC 说「改造格拉斯哥经济需要更严格的监管（Stricter regulation）」。录音里说话人主张\n的是 invest in the technologies and programmes（投资技术与项目），从头到尾没提\nregulation 或加强监管，C 属录音根本没说的内容。\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 盗用了录音 one of the only two low emission zones in action 里的 low emission\nzones。但说话人是把「已经在运行的低排放区」当作格拉斯哥的现有优势来陈述，并没有\n主张「有必要尽快多建低排放区（create more … as soon as possible）」，是把现状包装\n成主张的陷阱。\n借用原文：one of the only two low emission zones in action across the UK", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What do we learn from the news report about the Japanese IT firm?", "options": ["A) It donates money to overpopulated animal shelters.", "B) It permits employees to bring cats into their office.", "C) It gives 5,000 yen to employees who keep pet cats.", "D) It allows workers to do whatever their hearts desire."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It permits employees to bring cats into their office.\n【定位】Hidenobu Fukuda, who heads the firm, introduced the pet policy, upon request from one of\nhis employees, allowing staff to bring their own cats to work.\n公司负责人福田秀信应一名员工的请求，推出了这项宠物政策，允许员工把自己的猫带来\n上班。\n【信号】政策核心句直陈。这句用 allowing staff to bring their own cats to work 直接点明政策内\n容\"允许员工带猫上班\"，是新闻开头交代\"政策是什么\"的核心陈述句，无需转折，顺听\n到\"allowing staff…\"即可锁定。\n【替换】allowing staff to bring their own cats to work ↔\nIt permits employees to bring cats into their office\nallowing≈permits、staff≈employees\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n原文确实提到 shelters，也提到给钱，但给钱的对象是\"救猫的员工\"而非\"收容所\"。本项 It\ndonates money to overpopulated animal shelters. 把\"给员工\"偷换成\"给收容所\"，盗用\n了原文 overpopulated shelters 这个词来骗你。\n借用原文：rescue cats from overpopulated shelters or streets\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文的 5,000 yen 是发给\"救助了猫的人\"，不是发给\"养宠物猫的员工\"。本项 It gives\n5,000 yen to employees who keep pet cats. 照搬了 5,000 yen 这个醒目数字，却把领钱\n条件从\"rescue a cat\"换成\"keep pet cats\"，属听到数字就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：He gives 5,000 yen, about 45 US dollars a month to those who rescue a cat\nD) 原词陷阱\n原文 do whatever their little hearts desire 的主语是猫（furry friends），形容猫在办公\n室自由活动。本项 It allows workers to do whatever their hearts desire. 几乎原句照抄，\n却把主语从\"猫\"偷换成\"workers（员工）\"，是典型原词陷阱。\n借用原文：do whatever their little hearts desire", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does Fukuda encourage his employees to do?", "options": ["A) Keep cats off the street.", "B) Rescue homeless cats.", "C) Volunteer to help in animal shelters.", "D) Contribute to a fund for cat protection."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Rescue homeless cats.\n【定位】Not only does Fukuda encourage bringing cats to the job, but he also encourages his\nemployees to rescue cats from overpopulated shelters or streets.\n福田不仅鼓励员工把猫带来上班，还鼓励他们去救助来自爆满的收容所或街上的流浪猫。\n【信号】encourages领起的鼓励句。题干问 encourage 什么，原文里正好两次出现 encourage，\nanswer 落在 he also encourages his employees to 后面跟的动词 rescue cats from\noverpopulated shelters or streets，听到\"encourages…to rescue\"即可锁定。\n【替换】rescue cats from overpopulated shelters or streets ↔ Rescue homeless cats\nfrom shelters or streets≈homeless\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n原文说的是把街上的猫\"救回来（rescue from streets）\"，本项 Keep cats off the street.\n借用了\"街\"这个意象，却说成\"让猫别上街\"，动作方向正好相反。\n借用原文：rescue cats from overpopulated shelters or streets\nC) 原词陷阱\n本项 Volunteer to help in animal shelters. 盗用了原文出现的 shelters，但原文只说\"从收\n容所救猫\"，从没说鼓励员工\"去收容所做义工\"。听到 shelters 就选 C，正中原词陷阱。\n借用原文：rescue cats from overpopulated shelters or streets\nD) 偷换概念\n原文是\"公司给救猫的员工 5,000 日元\"，钱是公司发出去的；本项 Contribute to a fund\nfor cat protection. 却说成\"员工往护猫基金捐钱\"，把给钱方向倒置，且\"基金\"原文从未出\n现。\n借用原文：He gives 5,000 yen, about 45 US dollars a month to those who rescue a cat", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What does the news report say about the firm's policy?", "options": ["A) It has contributed tremendously to the firm's fame.", "B) It has helped a lot to improve animals' well-being.", "C) It has led some other companies to follow suit.", "D) It has resulted in damage to office equipment."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It has led some other companies to follow suit.\n【定位】Still, cats in the workplace have been a tremendous success for the company. The policy has\nled to various other companies doing the same.\n尽管如此，办公室养猫对这家公司而言已是巨大的成功。这项政策还促使其他多家公司纷纷\n效仿。\n【信号】结尾Still转折后的总评。末段以 Still 转折，把前一段的\"小麻烦\"翻过去，落到对政策的总\n评\"a tremendous success\"，紧接 The policy has led to various other companies\ndoing the same 是结果句——新闻结尾的转折+收束，正是答案所在。\n【替换】The policy has led to various other companies doing the same ↔\nIt has led some other companies to follow suit\ndoing the same≈follow suit\n【排除】A) 过度推断\n原文只说政策是 a tremendous success（巨大成功），并未说提升了公司\"名气\"。本项 It\nhas contributed tremendously to the firm's fame. 盗用了 tremendous 这个词，把\"成\n功\"擅自推成\"出名\"，属听到 tremendous 就想选的原词陷阱叠加过度推断。\n借用原文：cats in the workplace have been a tremendous success for the company\nB) 未提及\n本项 It has helped a lot to improve animals' well-being. 听着与\"救助流浪猫\"沾边，但\n新闻评价政策结果时讲的是\"对公司的成功\"和\"被别家效仿\"，并没有评价它\"改善了动物福\n祉\"，这句结论原文未提。\nD) 原词陷阱\n原文确有猫踩电话、踩关机键的内容，但那被归为 some obstacles（一些小障碍），且被\n末段 Still 转折掉，并非对政策的总体评价。本项 It has resulted in damage to office\nequipment. 盗用了\"电脑/电话\"这段细节冒充总评，属原词陷阱。\n借用原文：they shut down the computers by walking onto the off switch", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What does the man want to do?", "options": ["A) Find out where Jimmy is.", "B) Borrow money from Jimmy.", "C) Make friends with Jimmy.", "D) Ask Jimmy what is to be done."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Find out where Jimmy is.\n【定位】But I need to see him, because my friends and I lent him some money last month. We\nhaven't heard from him since. I heard he comes here a lot, so I thought I'd come in and check\nwith you.\n可我得见到他，因为上个月我和朋友们借给了他一些钱，之后就一直没他的消息。我听说他\n常来这儿，所以想着进来跟你打听一下。\n【信号】I need to see him 直陈来意。答案信号是男士直接道出目的的句子 I need to see him，\n加上 I thought I'd come in and check with you——\"来打听、确认他的下落\"。这是说话\n人主动交代意图的应答，听到 need to / I thought I'd 就该锁定。\n【替换】I thought I'd come in and check with you ↔ Find out where Jimmy is.\ncheck with you=find out where\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音里钱是男士他们\"借给\"Jimmy（lent him some money），不是向 Jimmy 借。选项\nBorrow money from Jimmy. 盗用了\"money\"和借钱情节，却把方向倒过来，答非所问。\n借用原文：lent him some money\nC) 未提及\n男士只想找到 Jimmy 把欠款的事说清，全程没有\"和 Jimmy 交朋友\"之意；Make friends\nwith Jimmy. 是凭空添加的。\nD) 偷换概念\n男士是来\"打听 Jimmy 在哪\"，不是要\"问 Jimmy 该怎么处理\"；Ask Jimmy what is to be\ndone. 借用了\"找 Jimmy、解决问题\"的氛围，却把\"找到他\"偷换成\"向他请教对策\"。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What do we learn about the man who came to see the woman yesterday?", "options": ["A) He was unsure what kind of fellow Jimmy was.", "B) He was working on a study project with Jimmy.", "C) He wanted to make a sincere apology to Jimmy.", "D) He wanted to invite her to join in a study project."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） He was working on a study project with Jimmy.\n【定位】Some other fellow came in asking for Jimmy just yesterday, a real nervous, pushy type of\nfellow, saying he needed Jimmy for some study project they were working on.\n就在昨天，另有一个家伙进来打听 Jimmy，一个特别紧张、咄咄逼人的家伙，说他需要\nJimmy 一起做他们正在搞的一个学习项目。\n【信号】some other fellow 引入新角色。答案信号是引入新人物的标志句 Some other fellow\ncame in asking for Jimmy just yesterday，紧接着 saying he needed Jimmy for some\nstudy project they were working on 就是对他的正面交代。听到\"another/some other\nfellow\"就知道题目问的人来了。\n【替换】he needed Jimmy for some study project they were working on ↔\nHe was working on a study project with Jimmy.\n几乎直述，仅换说法\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n对 Jimmy 是个什么人拿不准的是男士本人（后文 I did think it was a bit weird），不是昨\n天来的那个人；He was unsure what kind of fellow Jimmy was. 把感受安错了人头。\n借用原文：a real nervous, pushy type of fellow\nC) 原词陷阱\n\"道歉\"出现在女士对 Jimmy 撞车一事的猜测里（if you still wanted to give money as\nsome sort of apology），与昨天来的这个人无关；He wanted to make a sincere\napology to Jimmy. 盗用了 apology 一词错接到他身上。\n借用原文：some sort of apology\nD) 偷换概念\n录音说他需要 Jimmy 来做项目（he needed Jimmy for some study project），不是邀请\n女士加入；He wanted to invite her to join in a study project. 借用 study project 却把\n对象从 Jimmy 偷换成 her。\n借用原文：he needed Jimmy for some study project", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What did Jimmy say happened to him one day?", "options": ["A) He got a ticket for speeding.", "B) He got his car badly damaged.", "C) He was involved in a traffic accident.", "D) He had an operation for his injury."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He was involved in a traffic accident.\n【定位】He told us he'd got into an accident. Nothing serious, mind you, but he damaged someone's\ncar and wanted to get some money together to make up for the damage he'd caused.\n他跟我们说他出了一场事故。不严重，注意啊，但他撞坏了别人的车，想凑点钱来赔偿他造\n成的损失。\n【信号】He told us he'd got into an accident 转述。答案信号是男士转述 Jimmy 原话的句子\nHe told us he'd got into an accident——\"told us\"标明这是 Jimmy 说自己遇到的事，正\n对题干\"Jimmy say happened to him\"。听到\"He told us…\"就锁定。\n【替换】he'd got into an accident ↔ He was involved in a traffic accident.\ngot into=be involved in\n【排除】A) 未提及\nJimmy 只说出了事故、撞坏别人车，从没提\"超速被开罚单\"；He got a ticket for\nspeeding. 是凭空添加的情节。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音是 he damaged someone's car，撞坏的是\"别人的车\"；He got his car badly\ndamaged. 盗用 damaged/car，却把\"别人的车\"偷换成\"他自己的车\"，所属关系反了。\n借用原文：he damaged someone's car\nD) 过度推断\n录音明说 Nothing serious，受伤动手术更无从谈起；He had an operation for his injury.\n借\"accident\"联想到受伤手术，属过度推断。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "Why did Jimmy borrow money according to the man?", "options": ["A) He needed to make some donations to charity.", "B) He found the 60 pounds in his pocket missing.", "C) He wanted to buy a gift for his mother's birthday.", "D) He wanted to conceal something from his parents."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） He wanted to conceal something from his parents.\n【定位】But I thought maybe he doesn't want his parents to know about what happened. So he'd rather\ncome to us and keep things quiet.\n但我当时想，也许他是不想让父母知道这件事，所以他宁愿来找我们，把事情悄悄了结。\n【信号】But I thought…So… 男士给出原因。答案信号是转折 But 之后男士自己的推断 But I\nthought maybe he doesn't want his parents to know about what happened，加因果\nSo。题干限定 according to the man，而这正是男士（the man）给出的原因，转折+因\n果信号双重锁定。\n【替换】he doesn't want his parents to know about what happened ↔\nHe wanted to conceal something from his parents.\nnot want parents to know=conceal from parents\n【排除】A) 未提及\n全程没有\"捐款给慈善\"之说；He needed to make some donations to charity. 是凭空添\n加。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音里 just 60 pounds between the three of us 说的是三人借出的总额，不是 Jimmy\"口\n袋里丢了 60 镑\"；He found the 60 pounds in his pocket missing. 盗用数字 60 pounds\n编造\"丢钱\"情节。\n借用原文：just 60 pounds between the three of us\nC) 未提及\n借钱缘由是赔偿撞车、瞒着父母，从无\"为母亲生日买礼物\"；He wanted to buy a gift for\nhis mother's birthday. 凭空捏造。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What did the woman want to ask Max about?", "options": ["A) Shopping delivery.", "B) Shopping online.", "C) Where he goes shopping.", "D) How often he does shopping."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Shopping online.\n【定位】I wanted to ask you about ordering shopping online.\n我想问问你关于网上下单购物的事。\n【信号】开场\"I wanted to ask you about…\"直接点题。女方开口的 I wanted to ask you about\nordering shopping online 用 \"I wanted to ask you about…\" 这一典型的挑明话题句式，\n直接交代了她来请教的事，是开场点题信号。\n【替换】ordering shopping online ↔ Shopping online\nordering shopping online压缩为Shopping online\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n下文 I love getting my shopping delivered 反复出现 \"送货\" 一词，Shopping delivery\n正是盗这个原词。但女方开口请教的话题是 \"网购\"（online），送货只是网购的一个环节、\n是男方后面才展开的内容，并非她问的主题，答非所问。\n借用原文：I love getting my shopping delivered\nC) 答非所问\n录音结尾女方才问 which supermarket do you think is the best to order from（去哪家\n订），Where he goes shopping 像是套这处 \"where/哪家\"，但那是对话末尾她自己抛出\n的新问题，而非她一开始来请教的主题；且问的是 \"哪家超市\" 不是 \"他在哪买\"，方向也\n偏。\n借用原文：which supermarket do you think is the best to order from\nD) 未提及\nHow often he does shopping 问购物频率，但全篇没有任何关于 \"多久买一次\" 的内容，\n是利用选项里 shopping 一词凑出的无中生有项。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the man think is a problem with shopping in supermarkets?", "options": ["A) Searching in the aisles.", "B) Dealing with the traffic.", "C) Driving too long a distance.", "D) Getting one's car parked."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Getting one's car parked.\n【定位】It makes life so much simpler—no carrying heavy shopping bags in the rain, or trying to park\nthe car on those huge supermarket car parks then not being able to find it again after you\ncome out of the shops.\n（送货上门）让生活简单多了——不用在雨里拎沉甸甸的购物袋，也不用费劲把车停进超市\n那些巨大的停车场、逛完店出来又找不到车。\n【信号】他用\"再也不用……\"反衬出实体购物的麻烦。男方夸网购好时，用一串 \"no…\"、\"trying\nto… then not being able to…\" 反衬实体购物的烦恼，其中 trying to park the car on\nthose huge supermarket car parks then not being able to find it again 把 \"停车难、停\n完找不到车\" 当成最具体的一桩麻烦点出，这处对比式抱怨就是答案信号。\n【替换】trying to park the car on those huge supermarket car parks then not being able to find it again ↔\nGetting one's car parked\n把停车找车的长句概括为Getting one's car parked\n【排除】A) 未提及\nSearching in the aisles（在超市货架通道里找东西）是实体购物常见的麻烦联想，但录音\n里男方只抱怨了拎袋子和停车，从没提过 \"在货架间找东西\"，属凭常识补出来的未提及项。\nB) 偷换概念\n录音出现 park the car on those huge supermarket car parks，满是 car、car parks 等\n开车相关词，Dealing with the traffic 借这股 \"开车\" 气味把麻烦偷换成 \"应付交通/堵车\"，\n可录音说的是 \"停车找不到车\"，不是路上交通，概念被换了。\n借用原文：trying to park the car on those huge supermarket car parks\nC) 张冠李戴\nDriving too long a distance（开太远）像是搭后文 I live quite far away from my local\nsupermarket 的 \"住得远\"，但那是男方在第6段讲网购更省钱时提的省油背景，并非他这里\n点名的实体购物麻烦；本题问的麻烦是停车，不是开车距离，张冠李戴了。\n借用原文：I live quite far away from my local supermarket", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What is the woman's concern about shopping online?", "options": ["A) The after-sales service.", "B) The replacement policy.", "C) The quality of food products.", "D) The damage to the packaging."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The quality of food products.\n【定位】But there must be some problems. I mean, how do you know that you won't get bad fruit, or\nthat the eggs won't be broken?\n但肯定有些问题吧。我是说，你怎么知道不会收到坏掉的水果，或者鸡蛋不会碎呢？\n【信号】转折But+反问how do you know引出疑虑。女方用 But there must be some problems\n的转折先表态有顾虑，再用 how do you know that you won't get bad fruit 的反问点出\n具体担心，转折 But 加反问句是她抛出 concern 的双重信号。\n【替换】bad fruit, or that the eggs won't be broken ↔ The quality of food products\n坏水果\\碎鸡蛋上升为食品质量\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nThe after-sales service（售后服务）像是搭男方后文 they will give you the money back\nor replace the item 的处理措施，但那是男方解释 \"出问题怎么办\"，不是女方的担心；况\n且女方担心的是收到坏东西本身，不是售后，答非所问。\n借用原文：they will give you the money back or replace the item\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音男方说 replace the item（换货），The replacement policy（退换政策）正是盗\nreplace 这个原词。但那是男方的解决办法，不是女方的担心；女方的 concern 是食物质\n量，不是退换政策，原词陷阱。\n借用原文：they will give you the money back or replace the item\nD) 偷换概念\n女方提到 the eggs won't be broken（鸡蛋别碎），The damage to the packaging（包\n装破损）借这个 broken 把 \"食物本身坏/碎\" 偷换成 \"外包装破损\"。可她担心的是水果坏、\n蛋碎这种食物质量，不是包装，概念被悄悄换了。\n借用原文：the eggs won't be broken", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the man find about shopping online?", "options": ["A) It saves money.", "B) It offers more choice.", "C) It increases the joy of shopping.", "D) It is less time-consuming."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It saves money.\n【定位】I actually found that it was cheaper for me, as I live quite far away from my local\nsupermarket. So with the deliveries, I'm actually saving on petrol.\n我其实发现对我来说（网购）更便宜，因为我住得离本地超市挺远的。所以靠送货上门，我\n其实省下了油钱。\n【信号】I found that+cheaper\\saving的结论评价句。男方用 I actually found that it was\ncheaper for me 直接下结论，又用 I'm actually saving on petrol 点明省的是油钱，\"I\nfound that…\" 加比较级 cheaper、saving 是给网购下正面结论的答案信号。\n【替换】it was cheaper for me ↔ It saves money\ncheaper等于saves money\nI'm actually saving on petrol ↔ It saves money\nsaving on petrol是saves money的具体落点\n【排除】B) 未提及\nIt offers more choice（网购选择更多）是网购公认的好处之一，但录音里男方只讲了省\n钱、省力、食品一样好，从没提过 \"选择多\"，属凭常识补的未提及项。\nC) 过度推断\n男方开头说 I love getting my shopping delivered，It increases the joy of shopping\n（增加购物乐趣）像是顺着这个 \"love\" 推出来的，但 \"喜欢送货上门\" 不等于 \"购物变得更\n快乐\"，且本题问的是他 find 的好处，他明确 find 的是省钱，把喜好夸大成乐趣属过度推\n断。\n借用原文：I love getting my shopping delivered\nD) 偷换概念\n录音说网购 makes life so much simpler，It is less time-consuming（更省时）像是从\n\"生活更简单、不用拎袋停车\" 推出的省时，但 \"省力、简单\" 不等于 \"省时间\"，而他明确\nfind 的好处是 cheaper（省钱），把省力偷换成省时了。\n借用原文：It makes life so much simpler", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What did a recent study find about some British children?", "options": ["A) They have little talent for learning math.", "B) They need medical help for math anxiety.", "C) They need extra help to catch up in the math class.", "D) They have strong negative emotions towards math."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They have strong negative emotions towards math.\n【定位】A recent study found that 10 percent of British children suffer from math anxiety. This means\nthey have overwhelming negative emotions towards the subject, ranging from rage to despair.\n最近一项研究发现，10% 的英国孩子受数学焦虑困扰。这意味着他们对这门学科怀有压倒\n性的负面情绪，从暴怒到绝望不等。\n【信号】found 后接结论加 This means 解释。信号有两层：先是 A recent study found that 直\n接打出「研究发现」的旗号，这后面就是答案区；紧接着 This means they have\noverwhelming negative emotions towards the subject 用 This means 把「数学焦虑」\n翻译成大白话——正是这句「换种说法解释」给出了答案。听到 found 锁定区域、听到\nThis means 锁定那一句。\n【替换】overwhelming negative emotions towards the subject ↔ strong negative emotions towards math\noverwhelming=strong、the subject=math\nranging from rage to despair ↔ strong negative emotions\nrage 到 despair 是 negative emotions 的具体化\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nlittle talent for learning math 借了话题词 math 来制造熟悉感，但录音通篇讲的是「情\n绪」，从没说这些孩子「缺乏天赋」——恰恰相反，后文还强调他们能力中上。这是把「焦\n虑」想当然地等同于「没天赋」的常识误判。\n借用原文：suffer from math anxiety\nB) 原词陷阱\nmedical help for math anxiety 原样搬来录音的 math anxiety，又把后文的身体症状 a\nracing heart or struggling to catch breath 误读成「需要医疗帮助」。但录音只是描述症\n状，从没说这些孩子需要看病求医，属于借原词、推过头。\n借用原文：a racing heart or struggling to catch breath\nC) 未提及\nextra help to catch up in the math class 蹭了 math 这个话题，但「需要额外帮助跟上数\n学课」在录音里完全没有对应——录音讲的是情绪，不是学业进度落后。属于无中生有的干\n扰。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What is the widespread misunderstanding about math anxiety?", "options": ["A) It will gradually pass away without teachers' help.", "B) It affects low performing children only.", "C) It is related to a child's low intelligence.", "D) It exists mostly among children from poor families."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It affects low performing children only.\n【定位】There's a widespread misunderstanding that only low performing children suffer from math\nanxiety. People automatically assume children are anxious about math because they are\npoor achievers.\n存在一种普遍的误解：认为只有成绩差的孩子才会有数学焦虑。人们想当然地以为孩子焦虑\n是因为他们成绩差。\n【信号】widespread misunderstanding that 直接引出误解内容。信号词是 a widespread\nmisunderstanding that——它和题干用词几乎一模一样，that 后面那句就是误解的具体内\n容。紧跟的 People automatically assume 再用「人们想当然地以为」把同一误解复述一\n遍，进一步坐实。听到 misunderstanding that 就锁后半句。\n【替换】only low performing children suffer from math anxiety ↔\nIt affects low performing children only\nsuffer from 换成 affects，主被动对调\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIt will gradually pass away without teachers' help. 说焦虑会在没有老师帮助下自行消退\n——录音从没这么说，反而强调 in the long term their performance is negatively\naffected（长期看会损害成绩）。属于录音没提、且与原意相悖的编造。\nC) 偷换概念\nIt is related to a child's low intelligence. 借了录音 low performing 里的 low，把「成绩\n差（low performing / poor achievers）」偷换成了「智力低（low intelligence）」。录\n音谈的是学业表现，从没说焦虑跟智商有关，是典型的偷换概念。\n借用原文：only low performing children\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt exists mostly among children from poor families. 蹭了话题词 children，又把录音的\npoor achievers（成绩差的人）误读成 poor families（贫困家庭）——poor 一词两义被混\n用。录音从未把焦虑和家庭贫富挂钩，属误读原词。\n借用原文：they are poor achievers", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What does the passage say about British students with math anxiety?", "options": ["A) Most of them have average to strong math ability.", "B) Most of them get timely help from their teachers.", "C) They will regain confidence with counselling.", "D) They are mostly secondary school students."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Most of them have average to strong math ability.\n【定位】But importantly—and surprisingly—this new study suggests that the majority of students\nexperiencing math anxiety have normal to high math ability.\n但重要而且令人意外的是，这项新研究表明，大多数有数学焦虑的学生其实拥有正常到较高\n的数学能力。\n【信号】But 加 importantly 加 surprisingly 三重强调收尾。信号极强：But importantly—and\nsurprisingly 同时用了转折 but、强调 importantly、反直觉 surprisingly 三个标记叠在一\n起，等于作者在大喊「这句才是重点」。篇章独白里这种结尾的强调句几乎必是考点。听到\nbut 加 surprisingly，立刻把后面整句记下。\n【替换】the majority of students experiencing math anxiety ↔ Most of them\nthe majority of=Most of\nhave normal to high math ability ↔ have average to strong math ability\nnormal=average、high=strong\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nMost of them get timely help from their teachers. 录音恰恰相反：their math anxiety\nwill go unnoticed because their performance is good——因为成绩好，焦虑往往被忽\n视、得不到关注，谈何「及时得到老师帮助」。这是与原文相反的干扰。\n借用原文：their math anxiety will go unnoticed\nC) 未提及\nThey will regain confidence with counselling. 里的 counselling（心理咨询）在录音里\n从头到尾没出现过，「靠咨询恢复信心」完全是编造。录音只提到 a subsequent lack or\nloss of confidence（信心丧失），但从没说怎么靠咨询找回。\nD) 原词陷阱\nThey are mostly secondary school students. 借了录音 in both primary and secondary\nschool 里的 secondary school，却偷偷把「小学和中学都有」缩成「主要是中学生」。录\n音明说两个学段都受影响，选项却用 mostly 把范围卡死，属范围偷改。\n借用原文：both primary and secondary school", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What conclusion has the World Health Organization come to recently?", "options": ["A) Social media addiction is a threat to our health.", "B) Too many people are addicted to smartphones.", "C) Addiction to computer games is a disease.", "D) Computer games can be rather addictive."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Addiction to computer games is a disease.\n【定位】And now, the World Health Organization has recognized addiction to computer games as\na disease.\n而如今，世界卫生组织已经把电子游戏成瘾认定为一种疾病。\n【信号】主语复现 + now 标志新结论。录音里 And now, the World Health Organization has\nrecognized addiction to computer games as a disease. 同时出现了题干要找的主\n语\"World Health Organization\"和时间标志\"now\"（如今、最近），这正是题干 recently\n对应的那处\"新结论\"，是典型的答案句信号。\n【替换】recognized addiction to computer games as a disease ↔\nAddiction to computer games is a disease.\n\"认定…为疾病\"=\"…是疾病\"\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音第 1 段确实提到 social media、第 2 段提到这是健康相关的话题，A 就把\"social\nmedia\"和\"health\"拼成\"社交媒体成瘾威胁健康\"，但 WHO 下结论的对象是电子游戏成瘾、\n不是社交媒体，听到熟词就选会掉进这个坑。\n借用原文：text messages and social media\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音开头说 People spend a lot of time using phones and computers.，B 就顺势说\"太\n多人沉迷智能手机\"，可录音的结论针对的是\"游戏成瘾\"而非\"手机成瘾\"，且\"太多人\"是它没\n下过的数量判断，属于借原词制造的干扰。\n借用原文：People spend a lot of time using phones and computers.\nD) 与原文相反\nD Computer games can be rather addictive. 只是说游戏\"挺容易让人上瘾\"，这是第 2 段\n开头家长老师的担忧口吻 these games might be addictive，远没到 WHO\"认定为疾\n病\"的硬定性；问的是 WHO 的结论，就必须取那句最重的定性，故 D 程度偏轻、被排除。\n借用原文：these games might be addictive", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What is said of people with addictive behavior?", "options": ["A) They prioritize their favored activity over what they should do.", "B) They do their favored activity whenever and wherever possible.", "C) They are unaware of the damage their behavior is doing to them.", "D) They are unable to get rid of their addiction without professional help."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They prioritize their favored activity over what they should do.\n【定位】Such people make computer games a priority over their responsibilities.\n这类人把电子游戏看得比自己的责任更重要。\n【信号】指示词\"Such people\"领起对该类人的刻画。录音用 Such people make computer\ngames a priority over their responsibilities. 中的\"Such people\"（这类人）回指上文的\n成瘾者，正是题干\"people with addictive behavior\"指向的对象，这句对其行为的概括就\n是答案句信号。\n【替换】make computer games a priority over their responsibilities ↔\nThey prioritize their favored activity over what they should do.\n\"把游戏看得比责任重\"=\"把爱做的事置于该做的事之上\"\n【排除】B) 未提及\nB They do their favored activity whenever and wherever possible. 说成瘾者\"随时随地\n都玩\"，但录音从头到尾没说过\"whenever and wherever（任何时间任何地点）\"，它只说\n这些人拿游戏顶替了上学、工作、社交 They may play games instead of attending\nschool, or work, or socializing.，\"随时随地\"是凭常识脑补出来的。\nC) 原词陷阱\nC They are unaware of the damage their behavior is doing to them. 说成瘾者\"意识不\n到自己受的伤害\"，录音确实提到这种行为会 damage a person's relationships or\nperformance（损害关系或表现），但只说\"有危害\"，从没说成瘾者\"没意识到\"，C 把\"有危\n害\"偷换成了\"本人不自知\"。\n借用原文：damage a person's relationships or performance at school or work\nD) 未提及\nD They are unable to get rid of their addiction without professional help. 说成瘾\n者\"离了专业帮助戒不掉\"，录音通篇在讨论\"算不算病\"，从未提及\"professional help（专\n业帮助）\"或\"戒不掉\"，这是套用对\"成瘾\"的刻板印象添加的内容。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What do some behavior experts think of addiction to computer games?", "options": ["A) It may be less damaging than previously believed.", "B) There will never be agreement on its harm to people.", "C) It may prove to be beneficial to developing creativity.", "D) There is not enough evidence to classify it as a disease."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） There is not enough evidence to classify it as a disease.\n【定位】Still not all behavior experts agree. Some argue that there's not enough research on the\nsubject. Thus, they claim it is too early to call computer game addiction a disorder.\n不过，并非所有行为专家都赞同。有些专家认为这方面的研究还不够。因此，他们声称现在\n就把电子游戏成瘾称作一种障碍还为时过早。\n【信号】转折词 Still 领出反对声音。录音用 Still not all behavior experts agree. 中的\"Still\"（不\n过、然而）把语气从前文 WHO 的定论转向反方，紧接着的 Some argue that there's not\nenough research 才是这\"某些专家\"的真实立场——转折之后才是落点，这正是态度题最典\n型的答案信号。\n【替换】there's not enough research on the subject ↔\nThere is not enough evidence to classify it as a disease.\n\"研究不够\"=\"证据不足以定性为疾病\"\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA It may be less damaging than previously believed. 说\"危害可能比以前认为的小\"，录\n音里反方专家质疑的是\"研究够不够、能不能现在就定性\"，从没比较过\"危害大小\"，\ndamaging 一词虽在第 3 段出现过 damage a person's relationships，但\"less damaging\n（危害更小）\"是录音没有的比较。\n借用原文：damage a person's relationships or performance at school or work\nB) 过度推断\nB There will never be agreement on its harm to people. 说\"永远不会就其危害达成一\n致\"，录音只说现在\"not all…agree（并非都同意）\"、还需要更多研究，是\"暂时未达成一\n致\"，绝不是\"永远不会一致\"，never 把话推到了录音没有的极端。\n借用原文：Still not all behavior experts agree.\nC) 未提及\nC It may prove to be beneficial to developing creativity. 说游戏成瘾\"可能有益于培养创\n造力\"，录音通篇在争论\"算不算病\"，从未出现 beneficial（有益）或 creativity（创造力）\n任何相关内容，这是凭空添加的好处。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What do we learn about most European passports?", "options": ["A) They are relatively uniform in color and design.", "B) They appear more formal than other passports.", "C) They are a shade of red bordering on brown.", "D) They vary in color from country to country."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They are a shade of red bordering on brown.\n【定位】Switzerland's passport is dark red, for example, while most European countries prefer a shade\nof red. That's almost brown.\n举例来说，瑞士护照是深红色，而大多数欧洲国家偏爱一种红色，那种红几乎接近棕色。\n【信号】听到题干对象就锁定。录音念到 most European countries prefer a shade of red，正好\n对应题干的 most European passports，这就是答案句的信号——题干问什么对象，录\n音里点到这个对象的那一句即是答案句。紧接其后的 That's almost brown 补出近棕色的\n细节。\n【替换】most European countries prefer a shade of red ↔ They are a shade of red bordering on brown.\n原话原词搬进选项\nThat's almost brown ↔ bordering on brown\nalmost 与 bordering on 同义\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nrelatively uniform in color and design 借了原文反复出现的 color、design 话题词制造熟\n悉感，但录音说欧洲护照偏爱某种红色，并未说各国颜色与设计相对统一，属于听到原词就\n想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：slight variations\nB) 张冠李戴\nmore formal than other passports 把后文第 4 段讲深色封面显得正式 they appear\nmore official 张冠李戴到欧洲护照颜色这题上，且 formal 与 official 近义诱人，但本题问\n的是欧洲护照颜色，不是封面为何显正式。\n借用原文：they appear more official\nD) 与原文相反\nvary in color from country to country 与录音相反——录音强调 most European\ncountries prefer a shade of red，即多数欧洲国家偏爱同一种红色，是趋同而非各国不\n同，选项把意思反了。\n借用原文：most European countries prefer a shade of red", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What does the passage say about the design of passports?", "options": ["A) They must endure wear and tear.", "B) They must be of the same size.", "C) They must be made from a rare material.", "D) They must follow some common standards."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They must follow some common standards.\n【定位】There are no official rules regarding passport colors, but countries follow a certain set of\nnorms when designing them.\n关于护照颜色并没有官方规定，但各国在设计护照时会遵循一套特定的规范。\n【信号】转折 but 后是真意。There are no official rules 先说没有官方规定，but 之后才转到\ncountries follow a certain set of norms when designing them，转折 but 后面才是设计\n这一问的落点——前半的没有规定是干扰铺垫。\n【替换】follow a certain set of norms ↔ follow some common standards\nnorms 与 standards 同义\ncountries follow ↔ They must follow\n动词 follow 对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nendure wear and tear 里的磨损来自第 4 段 hide dirt and wear，录音确实念过 wear，但\n那是讲深色封面能藏脏污磨损，不是回答护照设计要遵守什么，听到 wear 就选即落入原词\n陷阱。\n借用原文：hide dirt and wear\nB) 原词陷阱\nof the same size 看似与第 2 段 size 沾边，但录音只说建议各国采用合适的类型、尺寸与\n样式，从未说护照必须大小相同，属借 size 一词的原词陷阱兼未提及。\n借用原文：size\nC) 原词陷阱\nmade from a rare material 借了第 3 段 made from a material 的 material 一词，但录音\n只要求材料能弯折，从未说稀有，rare 是凭空加上去的，属原词陷阱。\n借用原文：made from a material that bends", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "Why are dark colors chosen for passport covers?", "options": ["A) They look more traditional.", "B) They look more official.", "C) They are favored by airlines.", "D) They are easily identifiable."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They look more official.\n【定位】Simply put, countries stick to darker and more conservative colors because they appear\nmore official.\n简单说，各国坚持用更深、更保守的颜色，是因为这些颜色显得更正式、更官方。\n【信号】because 引出原因。录音 Simply put 提示要给结论，紧接 because they appear more\nofficial 用 because 直接交代原因——这正是 Why 题的答案信号，because 后面那句即是\n答案。\n【替换】they appear more official ↔ They look more official.\nappear 与 look 同义\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nlook more traditional 与正确项 look more official 仅一词之差，traditional（传统）与\nofficial（官方）近义易混，但录音 because 后说的是 more official，从未说 traditional，\n命题人就靠这对近义词诱你听岔。\n借用原文：more conservative colors\nC) 张冠李戴\nfavored by airlines 蹭了第 2 段提到的国际民航组织 The International Civil Aviation\nOrganization 这一航空背景，但录音从没说深色封面受航空公司青睐，把民航组织硬扯成\nairlines 偏好，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：The International Civil Aviation Organization\nD) 答非所问\neasily identifiable 是另起的一个特征，录音讲深色封面的原因是显官方、还能藏脏污磨损\nhide dirt and wear，从未说深色是为了易于辨识，与本题原因不符，属答非所问。\n借用原文：hide dirt and wear", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What is one special function of the images inside many passports?", "options": ["A) For beauty.", "B) For variety.", "C) For visibility.", "D) For security."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） For security.\n【定位】The passports of many countries can take images of their well-known geographical features\nand wildlife, which also function as security features as some are only visible under a\nspecial light.\n许多国家的护照内页印有本国著名的地理风貌和野生动物图案，这些图案还起到防伪作用，\n因为有些图案只在特殊光线下才能看到。\n【信号】function as 点出作用。录音 which also function as security features 用 function as 直\n接点明图案的作用是安全防伪——这正是问 function 一题的答案信号，function as 后面的\nsecurity features 即答案落点。后半的 only visible under a special light 是对这一安全功\n能的解释。\n【替换】function as security features ↔ For security.\nsecurity 原词即功能\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nFor beauty. 看似与内页印著名地理风貌、野生动物图案 images of their well-known\ngeographical features and wildlife 沾边，会让人觉得图案是为好看，但录音说的功能是\n安全防伪，从未说为了美观，属过度推断兼未提及。\n借用原文：images of their well-known geographical features and wildlife\nB) 答非所问\nFor variety. 蹭了录音讲各国内页图案不同、内页比封面有变化这层意思 the same cannot\nbe said for the pages，但那是说内页设计较多样，不是图案的功能，把内页多样性误当成\n图案功能，属答非所问。\n借用原文：the same cannot be said for the pages\nC) 原词陷阱\nFor visibility. 直接盗用录音 only visible under a special light 里的 visible 一词，且与正\n确项 For security 拼写相近极易混，但原文说的是图案只在特殊光下可见、这恰恰服务于安\n全防伪，可见只是手段、安全才是功能，听到 visible 就选即落入原词陷阱。\n借用原文：only visible under a special light", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abruptly", "B) appointments", "C) consequences", "D) debating", "E) dimensions", "F) friendly", "G) hindered", "H) idiom", "I) implication", "J) pushing", "K) severely", "L) sparked", "M) splitting", "N) survey", "O) touches"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) implication\n名词\n【词性槽】定冠词 The 后作主语的名词\n【依据】\"言外之意/启示是……\"\nThe __(26)__ is that loneliness is a huge, if silent, risk factor：承接首句\"孤独危害比肥胖\n吸烟还大\"的研究，这里说\"由此得出的含意／启示是：孤独是巨大却无声的风险因素\"，\nimplication（言外之意、启示）正是对前述研究结论的提炼，单数与 The...is 一致。\n【竞争词】E) dimensions 义不对且数不合\ndimensions 指\"维度\"，\"这个维度是孤独是风险因素\"语义不通；且 dimensions 为复数，与\n\"The ___ is\" 的单数 is 不一致。\nH) idiom 义不对：习语\nidiom（习语）与\"研究得出的结论/含意\"无关，\"这个习语是……\"读不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abruptly", "B) appointments", "C) consequences", "D) debating", "E) dimensions", "F) friendly", "G) hindered", "H) idiom", "I) implication", "J) pushing", "K) severely", "L) sparked", "M) splitting", "N) survey", "O) touches"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) appointments\n名词\n【词性槽】go to doctor 后接名词作宾语\n【依据】doctor appointments = 看病预约\ngo to doctor __(27)__：doctor appointments 是地道固定搭配，指\"去看医生的预约\"。本\n段在说独居者\"不太会去看病、运动或健康饮食\"，\"看病预约\"正好与 exercise、healthy\ndiet 并列成三件健康行为。\n【竞争词】E) dimensions 搭配不成立\n\"go to doctor dimensions\"（去看医生维度）不成句，dimensions 与 go to 不搭。\nN) survey 义不对：调查\n\"去做医生调查\"不合本句\"看病\"的语境；survey 留给空 34 更合适。\nH) idiom 义不对：习语\nidiom（习语）与\"去看病\"毫无关联。\nI) implication 义不对且已被占用\nimplication（含意）既不搭 go to doctor，又已是空 26 的答案。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abruptly", "B) appointments", "C) consequences", "D) debating", "E) dimensions", "F) friendly", "G) hindered", "H) idiom", "I) implication", "J) pushing", "K) severely", "L) sparked", "M) splitting", "N) survey", "O) touches"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) debating\n动词\n【词性槽】be 动词 are 后接 -ing 作谓语\n【依据】\"正在讨论如何应对\"\nexperts ... are __(28)__ how to address widespread loneliness：专家们\"正在争论／讨论\n如何应对孤独\"，debating（讨论、争论）后接 \"how to ...\" 地道，语义正是\"探讨对策\"，\n与下文\"英国甚至任命了孤独事务大臣\"的应对行动衔接。\n【竞争词】J) pushing 搭配指向不同\npushing 表\"推动、努力争取\"，常接 to do，不直接接 \"how to\" 表\"讨论怎么做\"；且 push 更\n贴空 33。\nM) splitting 义不对：分裂\n\"正在分裂如何应对孤独\"语义讲不通，splitting 与本句逻辑无关。\nO) touches 形态不符\ntouches 是单数现在时，无法跟在 are 后构成进行时，形态先被排除。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abruptly", "B) appointments", "C) consequences", "D) debating", "E) dimensions", "F) friendly", "G) hindered", "H) idiom", "I) implication", "J) pushing", "K) severely", "L) sparked", "M) splitting", "N) survey", "O) touches"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) touches\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 Loneliness 后缺谓语，宾语 us\n【依据】\"孤独影响着我们几乎每个人\"\nLoneliness touches almost every one of us at some point：touches 在此取\"触及、影\n响\"义，\"孤独在某些时刻几乎触及我们每一个人\"，单三形态与主语 Loneliness 一致，语义\n贴合\"孤独普遍\"这一全文论点。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abruptly", "B) appointments", "C) consequences", "D) debating", "E) dimensions", "F) friendly", "G) hindered", "H) idiom", "I) implication", "J) pushing", "K) severely", "L) sparked", "M) splitting", "N) survey", "O) touches"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) consequences\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 serious 后接名词\n【依据】\"导致严重后果\"是固定搭配\nlead to very serious health __(30)__：lead to（导致）后接的、又能被 serious（严重\n的）修饰的、还是讲健康层面的，正是 consequences（后果）。\"严重的健康后果\" 语义最\n顺，且与全文\"孤独危害健康\"的主旨完全咬合。\n【竞争词】E) dimensions 义不对：维度而非后果\ndimensions 指\"维度／方面\"，\"严重的健康维度\"不通；这里要的是 lead to 导出的结果，不是\n某个方面。\nI) implication 已被空 26 用掉\nimplication 是空 26 的答案，已从词库划走，按\"每词最多用一次\"不再参与。\nB) appointments 搭配不成立\nappointments 指\"预约\"，\"严重的健康预约\"讲不通，且它另有归属（空 27）。\nH) idiom 义不对：习语\nidiom 是\"习语\"，与 lead to 导致的健康结果毫无关系。\nN) survey 义不对：调查\nsurvey 是\"调查\"，\"严重的健康调查\"不合逻辑；它更贴空 34。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abruptly", "B) appointments", "C) consequences", "D) debating", "E) dimensions", "F) friendly", "G) hindered", "H) idiom", "I) implication", "J) pushing", "K) severely", "L) sparked", "M) splitting", "N) survey", "O) touches"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) sparked\n动词\n【词性槽】that 引导定语从句缺谓语\n【依据】\"引发了……对话\"\ncampaign that __(31)__ difficult conversations across Britain：一场运动\"在英国各地引\n发／激起了难以启齿的对话\"，sparked（引发、激起）与 conversations 搭配地道，\nsparked discussions/conversations 是高频组合，语义指向\"运动带来积极效果\"。\n【竞争词】O) touches 时态与搭配都差\ntouches 是单数现在时，与本段过去语境不符，且\"触及对话\"不如\"引发对话\"自然；touches 更\n贴空 29。\nG) hindered 逻辑指向相反\nhindered（阻碍）意思反了——运动是要促进而非阻碍交流，与积极主旨冲突。\nM) splitting 形态与义都不对\nsplitting 是 -ing 形式不能直接作过去式谓语，且\"分裂对话\"语义怪异。\nJ) pushing 形态不合且另有归属\npushing 为 -ing 形式，作 that 从句谓语需 pushed，形态不对；且 push 更贴空 33 的\n\"pushing to\"。\nD) debating 形态不合且另有归属\ndebating 为 -ing 形式，此处需过去式谓语；debating 更贴空 28 的进行时结构。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 G) —— hindered 形态对、逻辑反。空 31 要过去式动词，\nhindered 形态完全合规，光看语法分不出对错。防坑：回到语义方向——\"Let's Talk\nLoneliness\" 运动是要\"引发\"对话而非\"阻碍\"对话，逻辑指向决定选 sparked。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abruptly", "B) appointments", "C) consequences", "D) debating", "E) dimensions", "F) friendly", "G) hindered", "H) idiom", "I) implication", "J) pushing", "K) severely", "L) sparked", "M) splitting", "N) survey", "O) touches"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) friendly\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词 benches 前作定语\n【依据】\"友好长椅\"鼓励陌生人交谈\n\"friendly benches,\" which are public seating areas where people are encouraged to\ngo and chat with one another：后面的定语从句解释这是\"鼓励人们去和彼此聊天的公共\n座椅\"，正好印证 friendly（友好的）——\"友好长椅\"，主题是化解孤独、促进交流。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abruptly", "B) appointments", "C) consequences", "D) debating", "E) dimensions", "F) friendly", "G) hindered", "H) idiom", "I) implication", "J) pushing", "K) severely", "L) sparked", "M) splitting", "N) survey", "O) touches"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) pushing\n动词\n【词性槽】be 动词 is 后接 -ing，再接 to do\n【依据】push to do = 努力争取做某事\nis also __(33)__ to stop public transportation from being cut：pushing to stop（努力\n争取阻止）地道，\"大臣还在努力争取阻止削减公共交通\"，push to do 是固定搭配，语义\n指向\"主动推动政策\"，与上文\"支持友好长椅\"的积极行动并列。\n【竞争词】M) splitting 搭配与义都不对\nsplitting 不与 to do 构成\"努力做某事\"的搭配，且\"分裂去阻止\"语义讲不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abruptly", "B) appointments", "C) consequences", "D) debating", "E) dimensions", "F) friendly", "G) hindered", "H) idiom", "I) implication", "J) pushing", "K) severely", "L) sparked", "M) splitting", "N) survey", "O) touches"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) survey\n名词\n【词性槽】冠词 a 后、介词 that 从句前的名词\n【依据】\"在一项调查中表示\"\nsaid in a 2018 __(34)__ that they often or always feel lonely：人们\"在 2018 年的一项调\n查中表示……\"，survey（调查）正是承载\"超五分之一成年人说常感孤独\"这类数据结论的载\n体，搭配 said in a survey 地道自然。\n【竞争词】I) implication 义不对且已被占用\nimplication（含意）不是人们\"在其中说话\"的场合，且已是空 26 答案。\nE) dimensions 义不对：维度\n\"在一个 2018 维度中说\"不通，dimensions 与 said in 不搭。\nH) idiom 义不对：习语\nidiom（习语）无法承载调查数据，语义不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abruptly", "B) appointments", "C) consequences", "D) debating", "E) dimensions", "F) friendly", "G) hindered", "H) idiom", "I) implication", "J) pushing", "K) severely", "L) sparked", "M) splitting", "N) survey", "O) touches"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) severely\n副词\n【词性槽】修饰形容词 strained 的状语\n【依据】\"严重紧张\"程度副词\n30% of relationships are __(35)__ strained：severely（严重地）修饰 strained，\"30%\n的关系严重紧张\"，程度副词与\"strained（紧张）\"语义同向、自然加强，呼应本段\"即便已\n婚也有大量关系出问题\"的论点。\n【竞争词】A) abruptly 义不对：突然 vs 程度\nabruptly 表\"突然地\"，强调方式/时间，不是程度；\"突然紧张\"与 strained 这种持续状态不搭，\n这里要的是\"紧张的严重程度\"。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 A) —— abruptly 词性对、易抢答。空 35 副词只有 abruptly 与\nseverely 两候选，词性都对，慌乱中可能凭眼熟选 abruptly。防坑：先问空修饰的是\"程\n度\"还是\"方式\"——are __(35)__ strained 要的是\"严重程度\"，选程度副词 severely。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "It is difficult for anumeric people to keep track of the change in numbers even when the total is very small.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】数量很小也跟不上变化\n【定位】Responses suggest that anumeric people have some trouble keeping track of how many nuts\nremain in the can, even if there are only four or five in total.\n实验结果表明，无数字者很难追踪罐子里还剩多少颗坚果，即便总共只有四到五颗。\n【改写】keep track of the change in numbers ↔ keeping track of how many nuts remain in the can\n具体数→抽象数的上位替换\neven when the total is very small ↔ even if there are only four or five in total\n模糊量词替换具体数字\n【辨邻】 E seven or eight coconuts（椰子）in a tree是静态计数，缺少“放进去再拿出来”的动\n态追踪，无法覆盖陈题里的keep track of the change。\nwithout counting, how can someone tell whether there are, say, seven or eight coconuts（椰子）in\na tree?", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Human numerical instincts are not so superior to those of other mammals as is generally believed.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】与其他哺乳动物的对比\n【定位】Compared with other mammals, our numerical instincts are not as remarkable as\nmany assume.\n与其他哺乳动物相比，我们的数字本能并没有许多人以为的那么了不起。\n【改写】Human numerical instincts ↔ our numerical instincts\n代词替换\nnot so superior to those of other mammals ↔ not as remarkable as many assume\n形容词同义替换\nas is generally believed ↔ as many assume\n主动到被动 + 近义词\n【辨邻】 G Human brains come equipped with certain quantitative instincts侧重“人本能有\n限、需要后天培养”，并未与其他哺乳动物作比较，不能承载陈题的对比含义。\nHuman brains come equipped with certain quantitative instincts that are refined with age, but these\ninstincts are very limited.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "The author emphasizes being anumeric does not affect one's cognitive ability.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】无数字不影响认知能力\n【定位】It is worth stressing that these anumeric people are cognitively（在认知方面）normal, well-\nadapted to the surroundings they have dominated for centuries.\n值得强调的是，这些无数字者在认知上完全正常，并且对自己掌控了数个世纪的环境适应\n良好。\n【改写】The author emphasizes ↔ It is worth stressing\n强调信号词同义替换\nbeing anumeric does not affect one's cognitive ability ↔\nthese anumeric people are cognitively（在认知方面）normal\n正反表述等价\n【辨邻】 G Human brains come equipped with certain quantitative instincts...but these\ninstincts are very limited谈的是普遍本能有限，与“无数字者认知正常”这一具体强调\n不在一个层面。\nthese instincts are very limited\nC Without numbers, healthy human adults struggle to precisely distinguish and\nrecall quantities as low as four是在交代实验背景，并未出现“It is worth stressing /\ncognitively normal”这种作者强调的表达。\nWithout numbers, healthy human adults struggle to precisely distinguish and recall quantities as\nlow as four.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "In the long history of mankind, humans who use numbers are a very small minority.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】漫长历史里使用数字者是极少数\n【定位】But, in a historical sense, number-conscious people like us are the unusual ones.\n但是，从历史的角度看，像我们这样有数字意识的人才是异常的少数。\n【改写】In the long history of mankind ↔ in a historical sense\n历史视角对应\nhumans who use numbers are a very small minority ↔\nnumber-conscious people like us are the unusual ones\n异类即少数\n【辨邻】 D While only a small portion of the world's languages are anumeric or nearly\nanumeric说的是无数字语言在所有语言中占比小，与陈题“历史长河中使用数字者占少\n数”含义相反。\nWhile only a small portion of the world's languages are anumeric or nearly anumeric", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "An in-depth study of differences between human languages contributes to a true understanding of cognitive differences between cultures.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】深入研究语言差异以理解认知差异\n【定位】If we are to truly understand how much our cognitive lives differ cross-culturally, we must\ncontinually explore the depths of our species' linguistic diversity.\n如果我们想真正理解人类的认知生活在不同文化之间差异有多大，就必须持续深入探索我们\n这一物种的语言多样性。\n【改写】An in-depth study of differences between human languages ↔\nexplore the depths of our species' linguistic diversity\n名词化与动词化的对换\ncontributes to a true understanding of cognitive differences between cultures ↔\nIf we are to truly understand how much our cognitive lives differ cross-culturally\n条件句转“有助于”\n【辨邻】 D they demonstrate that number words are not a human universal只是事实陈述，\n没有上升到“通过研究语言差异理解跨文化认知差异”的总论。\nthey demonstrate that number words are not a human universal", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "A conclusion has been drawn from many experiments that anumeric people have a hard time distinguishing quantities.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】由大量实验得出的结论\n【定位】This and many other experiments have led to a simple conclusion: When people do not have\nnumber words, they struggle to make quantitative distinctions that probably seem natural to\nsomeone like you or me.\n这个实验和许多其他实验得出了一个简单的结论：当人们没有数字词时，他们就很难做出在\n你我看来或许稀松平常的量化区分。\n【改写】A conclusion has been drawn from many experiments ↔\nThis and many other experiments have led to a simple conclusion\n主动到被动\nanumeric people have a hard time distinguishing quantities ↔\nthey struggle to make quantitative distinctions\n短语同义替换\n【辨邻】 C In an experiment, a researcher will place nuts into a can只讲了一个具体实验，并\n未提出“多项实验得出一个总结论”这一总览式判断。\nIn an experiment, a researcher will place nuts into a can one at a time and then remove them one\nby one.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Making quantitative distinctions is not an inborn skill.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】量化区分非天生\n【定位】We are not born to handle quantitative distinctions skillfully.\n我们并非天生就能熟练地做量化区分。\n【改写】Making quantitative distinctions ↔ handle quantitative distinctions skillfully\n动词替换\nnot an inborn skill ↔ We are not born to\n形容词与不定式的等值\n【辨邻】 F acquiring the exact meaning of number words is a painstaking process that\ntakes children years描述的是后天习得的难度过程，不是直接对“天生能力”的否定\n判断。\nacquiring the exact meaning of number words is a painstaking process that takes children years\nH our numerical instincts are not as remarkable as many assume是与其他物种相\n比，而陈题强调“在人内部，量化区分非天生”，落点不同。\nour numerical instincts are not as remarkable as many assume", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Every aspect of our lives is affected by numbers.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】生活方方面面被数字影响\n【定位】The exact numbers we think with impact everything in our lives.\n我们头脑里所用的那些精确数字影响着我们生活的方方面面。\n【改写】Every aspect of our lives is affected by numbers ↔\nThe exact numbers we think with impact everything in our lives\n全称量词 + 近义动词 + 主被动转换\n【辨邻】 J Your day is ruled by minutes and seconds虽涉及日常被数字管，但只覆盖时间这一\n个维度，不足以对位陈题的“every aspect”。\nYour day is ruled by minutes and seconds", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Larger numbers are said to be built upon smaller numbers.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】大数建立在小数之上\n【定位】That is, these smaller numbers are the basis of larger numbers.\n也就是说，这些较小的数字是较大数字的基础。\n【改写】Larger numbers are said to be built upon smaller numbers ↔\nthese smaller numbers are the basis of larger numbers\n主客易位 + 介词关系替换\n【辨邻】 J base-60 number system used in ancient Mesopotamia只交代了某进制的来源，没\n有“小数构成大数”的关系说明。\nbase-60 number system used in ancient Mesopotamia", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "It takes great efforts for children to grasp the concept of number words.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】孩子掌握数字词需付出巨大努力\n【定位】In fact, acquiring the exact meaning of number words is a painstaking process that takes\nchildren years.\n事实上，孩子要真正掌握数字词的确切含义，是一个让他们要花上数年的艰苦过程。\n【改写】It takes great efforts for children ↔ a painstaking process that takes children years\n“painstaking + takes years”=“great efforts”\ngrasp the concept of number words ↔ acquiring the exact meaning of number words\n动词同义替换\n【辨邻】 G Number words and their written forms transform our quantitative reasoning as\nthey are introduced into our cognitive experience by our parents, peers and\nschool teachers虽然提及孩子的学习来源，但落点在“后天获得”，并未刻画孩子掌握\n数字词的“数年艰辛”。\nNumber words and their written forms transform our quantitative reasoning as they are introduced\ninto our cognitive experience by our parents, peers and school teachers.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What did Harvard scientists do 50 years ago?", "options": ["A) They raised public awareness of the possible causes of heart disease.", "B) They turned public attention away from the health risks of sugar to fat.", "C) They placed the sugar industry in the spotlight with their new findings.", "D) They conducted large-scale research on the role of sugar in people's health."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What did Harvard scientists do 50 years ago? 问的是过去某个具体行为，答案应是定位\n段对该行为的客观陈述。\n【拆句】What did Harvard scientists do 50 years ago? 是 what 引导的一般疑问句，主语\nHarvard scientists，时间状语 50 years ago，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Sugar shocked. That describes the reaction of many Americans this week following\nrevelations that, 50 years ago, the sugar industry paid Harvard scientists for research that\nshifted the focus away from sugar's role in heart disease—and put the spotlight(注意的中心)\nsquarely on dietary fat.\n糖业震惊。这描述了本周许多美国人在得知一桩内幕后的反应——50 年前，糖业出钱请哈\n佛科学家做研究，把心脏病的责任焦点从糖身上挪开，狠狠地压在了膳食脂肪头上。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nraised public awareness of the possible causes of heart disease 说哈佛科学家\"提高\n了公众对心脏病病因的认知\"，但原文只说他们\"转移焦点\"，从未提及\"提高认知\"，且行\n为方向相反——是误导而非启蒙。\nB) ✓ 正确\nshifted the focus away from sugar's role in heart disease—and put the spotlight(注意的中心)\nsquarely on dietary fat\n↔ turned public attention away from the health risks of sugar to fat\n预判内容\"把心脏病矛头从糖转向脂肪\"与 turned public attention away from the\nhealth risks of sugar to fat 在框架、对象、方向上完全重合，即同义替换型正确项。\nC) 与原文相反\nplaced the sugar industry in the spotlight with their new findings 说研究让\"糖\n业\"成为焦点，与原文 put the spotlight(注意的中心) squarely on dietary fat 恰恰相\n反——焦点是脂肪而非糖业。\nD) 无中生有\nconducted large-scale research on the role of sugar in people's health 强调\"大规\n模\"研究，但原文未提及研究规模，只说糖业出钱资助；且研究内容不是\"糖与健康的作\n用\"而是\"转移焦点\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does Marion Nestle say about present-day nutrition studies?", "options": ["A) They took her a full year to track and analyze.", "B) Most of them are based on systematic reviews.", "C) They depend on funding from the food industries.", "D) Nearly all of them serve the purpose of the funders."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does Marion Nestle say about present-day nutrition studies? 直接索取一个人对\n一事的具体说法，答案应是文中该人原话的转述。\n【拆句】What does Marion Nestle say about present-day nutrition studies? 主干 What...does\nMarion Nestle say，about 引出宾语补足语限定 say 的内容范围；句法直白。\n【定位】\"Roughly 90% of nearly 170 studies favored the sponsor's interest,\" Nestle tells us.\nNestle 告诉我们：\"在近 170 项研究里，约 90% 的结果都偏向了资助方的利益。\"\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nThey took her a full year to track and analyze 说\"研究花了她一整年追踪\"，对应原文\nspent a year informally tracking，但这是关于 Nestle 的研究方法，不是她\n对\"present-day nutrition studies\"本身的看法，答非所问。\nB) 张冠李戴\nMost of them are based on systematic reviews 把\"systematic reviews\"安\n到\"present-day nutrition studies\"头上，但原文 Other systematic reviews support\nher conclusions 是说\"其他系统性综述支持 Nestle 的结论\"，systematic reviews 是支\n持她的工具，不是被她评论的研究的本质。\nC) 偷换概念\nThey depend on funding from the food industries 说\"研究依赖食品业资助\"，是\"资\n金来源\"层面的事实，而原文 Nestle 强调的是\"研究结论偏向资助方\"——把\"结论偏\n袒\"偷换成了\"资金依赖\"。两者层面不同。\nD) ✓ 正确\nRoughly 90% of nearly 170 studies favored the sponsor's interest ↔\nNearly all of them serve the purpose of the funders\n预判\"九成研究服务资助方利益\"与 Nearly all of them serve the purpose of the\nfunders 在数量级、动作、对象三层全部对齐，是教科书级同义替换正确项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What did Coca-Cola-funded studies claim?", "options": ["A) Exercise is more important to good health than diet.", "B) Choosing what to eat and drink is key to weight control.", "C) Drinking Coca-Cola does not contribute to weight gain.", "D) The food industry plays a major role in fighting obesity."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What did Coca-Cola-funded studies claim? 问被可口可乐资助的研究主张了什么，答案\n是文中明示的论断。\n【拆句】What did Coca-Cola-funded studies claim? 主语 Coca-Cola-funded studies 是\"名词-\nfunded 复合形容词+名词\"结构，意为\"被可口可乐资助的研究\"；句意直白。\n【定位】Last year, The New York Times revealed how Coca-Cola was funding well-known scientists\nand organizations promoting a message that, in the battle against weight gain, people should\npay more attention to exercise and less to what they eat and drink.\n去年《纽约时报》披露，可口可乐资助知名科学家和组织，宣扬这样一种主张：在对抗体重\n增长的战斗中，人们应更看重运动，而不是吃了什么、喝了什么。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nin the battle against weight gain, people should pay more attention to exercise and less to what\nthey eat and drink\n↔ Exercise is more important to good health than diet\n预判\"重运动而轻饮食\"与 Exercise is more important to good health than diet 的\"运\n动比饮食更重要\"严格等价；定位句的 more/less 平行结构本身就是隐性比较。\nB) 与原文相反\nChoosing what to eat and drink is key to weight control 说\"吃喝选择是控重关键\"，\n与原文 less to what they eat and drink\"少在乎吃喝\"完全相反——可口可乐研究恰恰\n要弱化饮食责任。\nC) 无中生有\nDrinking Coca-Cola does not contribute to weight gain 凭空捏造一句\"喝可乐不会\n增重\"，原文从未出现此论断；可口可乐资助研究只是\"转移注意力\"到运动，并未直接为\n可乐本身辩护。\nD) 偷换概念\nThe food industry plays a major role in fighting obesity 说\"食品业在抗肥胖中起重\n要作用\"，看似与原文相关，但原文是说\"可口可乐资助研究让人忽视饮食\"，并未把食品\n业塑造为\"抗肥胖功臣\"；把\"产业操纵议程\"偷换成了\"产业贡献抗肥胖\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does Liebman say about industry-funded research?", "options": ["A) It simply focuses on nutrition and health.", "B) It causes confusion among consumers.", "C) It rarely results in objective findings.", "D) It runs counter to the public interest."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does Liebman say about industry-funded research? 同 Q47 同款句式，仍是细节\n题，定位到 Liebman 引语即可。\n【拆句】What does Liebman say about industry-funded research? 结构同 Q47；industry-\nfunded 是\"名词-过去分词\"复合形容词，表被动\"被产业资助的\"。\n【定位】\"When the food industry pays for research, it often gets what it pays for.\" And what it pays\nfor is often a pro-industry finding.\n\"当食品业出钱做研究时，它常常能买到自己想要的结论。\"而它想买的，往往是一个对产业\n有利的结果。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nIt simply focuses on nutrition and health 只说研究\"聚焦营养健康\"，是话题描述，未\n触及 Liebman 对\"业内资助偏袒\"的核心评价；属答非所问。\nB) 无中生有\nIt causes confusion among consumers 说\"造成消费者困惑\"，原文 P5 Liebman 谈的\n是\"研究结论偏袒产业\"，未提及消费者困惑这一后果——\"困惑\"是后文（P6）作者建议\n引出的语境，但不是 Liebman 的直接评价。\nC) ✓ 正确\nwhat it pays for is often a pro-industry finding ↔ It rarely results in objective findings\nWhen the food industry pays for research, it often gets what it pays for ↔\nIt rarely results in objective findings\n预判\"买到的多是偏袒产业的结论\"与 It rarely results in objective findings 是同一事实\n的正反描述：常偏袒=极少客观。\nD) 过度推断\nIt runs counter to the public interest 跨越\"研究偏袒产业\"直接推到\"与公众利益对\n立\"——这是合理但过度的引申。原文只说结论亲产业，没有明示\"对立于公众利益\"，\n且 Liebman 所在机构名 Center for Science in the Public Interest 是机构名而非她的\n论断。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What is the author's advice to consumers?", "options": ["A) Follow their intuition in deciding what to eat.", "B) Be doubtful of diet experts' recommendations.", "C) Ignore irrelevant information on their news feed.", "D) Think twice about new nutrition research findings."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is the author's advice to consumers? 问的是一项具体行动建议，答案是文末作者\n明示的劝告，仍属细节题（非笼统主旨）。\n【拆句】What is the author's advice to consumers? What is 主系表结构，主语 the author's\nadvice，介词短语 to consumers 修饰 advice 表对象；无难点。\n【定位】Given this environment, consumers should be skeptical(怀疑的) when reading the latest\nfinding in nutrition science and ignore the latest study that pops up on your news feed.\n鉴于这种环境，消费者读到营养科学的最新发现时应当保持怀疑，并对新闻推送里冒出来的\n最新研究置之不理。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nFollow their intuition in deciding what to eat 让消费者\"凭直觉决定吃什么\"，与原文\n末段 Rely on health experts who've reviewed all the evidence（依赖审过全部证据\n的健康专家）方向相反——作者要的是依赖权威而非凭直觉。\nB) 与原文相反\nBe doubtful of diet experts' recommendations 让消费者\"怀疑膳食专家的建议\"，但\n原文恰恰相反 Rely on health experts who've reviewed all the evidence——要怀疑\n的是\"业内出资的最新研究\"，不是\"健康专家\"。\nC) 偷换概念\nIgnore irrelevant information on their news feed 让消费者\"忽略新闻推送里的无关信\n息\"，但原文 ignore the latest study that pops up on your news feed 让消费者忽略\n的是\"新闻推送里冒出的最新研究\"——把\"最新研究\"偷换成\"无关信息\"，对象被偷偷扩大\n并改性质。\nD) ✓ 正确\nconsumers should be skeptical(怀疑的) when reading the latest finding in nutrition science ↔\nThink twice about new nutrition research findings\n预判\"对新研究保持怀疑\"与 Think twice about new nutrition research findings 在态\n度（怀疑↔三思）与对象（最新营养发现↔新营养研究结论）两层都对齐。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author use the example of his father to illustrate?", "options": ["A) How long people took to reach the top of their career.", "B) How people accumulated wealth in his father's time.", "C) How people viewed success in his father's time.", "D) How long people usually stayed in a company."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （例证目的题）\n【判型】题干What does the author use the example of his father to illustrate?里use the\nexample...to illustrate是例证目的题的标志句式。\n【拆句】What does the author use the example of his father to illustrate?主干是What does\nthe author use...to illustrate，宾语the example of his father插在use和to illustrate之\n间，可还原为The author uses the example of his father to illustrate what？句子结构清\n晰，无陷阱。\n【定位】Success was once defined as being able to stay at a company for a long time and move up the\ncorporate ladder. The goal was to reach the top, accumulate wealth and retire to a life of ease.\nMy father is a successful senior executive. In 35 years, he worked for only three companies.\n成功曾经被定义为能在一家公司长期任职、并沿企业晋升阶梯向上攀升。其目标是登上顶\n峰、积累财富、退休享福。我父亲是一位成功的高级主管。35年里他只为三家公司工作过。\n【选项】A) 把例子局部细节当成例证目的=以偏概全\nA项How long people took to reach the top of their career.只截取了例子里\"长期任\n职\"这层片面信息，且\"花多久到顶\"是文中未直接给出的时间细节。\nB) 拎走并列项里的一个=以偏概全\nB项How people accumulated wealth in his father's time.把原文里accumulate\nwealth截出来做主旨，但accumulate wealth只是论点中目标的一个并列项，并非例子\n要说明的核心。\nC) ✓ 正确\nSuccess was once defined as being able to stay at a company for a long time and move up the\ncorporate ladder.\n↔ How people viewed success in his father's time.\nC项把\"父辈时代人们如何看待成功\"完整对应原文论点句的语义，例子（35年三家公司）\n正是这个看法的证据。\nD) 把例子字面=目的，是答非所问\nD项How long people usually stayed in a company.几乎是父亲例子（he worked for\nonly three companies 35年）的复述，问的是\"例子表面讲了啥\"，而题问的是\"例子用\n来证明啥\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "例证目的题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "Why did people often change jobs when the author started his career?", "options": ["A) It was considered a fashion at that time.", "B) It was a way to advance in their career.", "C) It was a response to the changing job market.", "D) It was difficult to keep a job for long."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】Why did people often change jobs when the author started his career?问的是当时换\n工作背后的具体原因，答案能在原文里找到对应表述。\n【拆句】Why did people often change jobs when the author started his career?主干Why did\npeople change jobs，后接when引导的时间状语从句，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】If you weren't changing companies every three or four years, you simply weren't getting\nahead in your career.\n如果你没在每三四年换一次公司，那你的职业基本就没在前进。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nA项It was considered a fashion at that time.里的fashion（流行风尚）在原文中无任\n何对应表述，作者强调的是\"不换就升不上去\"的功利动因，而非流行潮流。\nB) ✓ 正确\nIf you weren't changing companies every three or four years, you simply weren't getting ahead\nin your career.\n↔ It was a way to advance in their career.\n从预判\"换工作是为了职业前进\"出发，B项直接以a way to advance in their career对\n位，完全覆盖定位句的正面含义。\nC) 偷换概念\nC项It was a response to the changing job market.把换工作说成是对\"职场变化\"的被\n动响应，原文给出的是主动追求职业前进的内在动因，逻辑方向相反。\nD) 与原文相反\nD项It was difficult to keep a job for long.说当时\"很难长期保住一份工作\"，原文说的\n是\"如果想前进就得自己换\"，并非工作保不住；D项把主动换岗读成被动失业。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does the author say about people now working for several businesses at the same time?", "options": ["A) They are often regarded as most treasured talents.", "B) They are able to bring their potential into fuller play.", "C) They have control over their life and work schedules.", "D) They feel proud of being outstanding problem solvers."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about people now working for several businesses at the\nsame time?问的是作者对该群体的具体评价，原文应有直接陈述。\n【拆句】What does the author say about people now working for several businesses at the\nsame time?主干What does the author say about people，working for several\nbusinesses at the same time是people的现在分词后置定语；now是时间副词，把范围限\n定在当下。\n【定位】Today, consulting or freelancing for five businesses at the same time is a badge of honor. It\nshows how valuable an individual is.\n如今，同时为五家公司做咨询或自由职业是一种荣誉徽章，它显示一个人有多么有价值。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nIt shows how valuable an individual is. ↔\nThey are often regarded as most treasured talents.\na badge of honor ↔ are often regarded as\n从预判出发，A项把badge of honor+how valuable汇总成most treasured talents，\n最贴合作者对这群人的评价。\nB) 张冠李戴（错段拼接）\nB项They are able to bring their potential into fuller play.说他们\"能把潜力发\n挥得更充分\"，原文未在53题的定位段说这件事，潜力发挥要到P4再讨论focused\nand productive。\nC) 过度推断\nC项They have control over their life and work schedules.说他们\"掌控自己的生活和\n工作日程\"，原文虽提到为多家公司打工，但没说他们对日程有控制权——这层引申是读\n者的主观联想。\nD) 偷换概念（叙述视角+部分原因）\nD项They feel proud of being outstanding problem solvers.说他们\"为自己作为解决\n问题者而自豪\"，原文里badge of honor是社会对他们的视角，不是他们自己feel\nproud；problem solvers也只是企业聘他们的部分原因。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What have businesses come to recognize now?", "options": ["A) Who is capable of solving problems with ease.", "B) How people can be more focused and productive.", "C) What kind of people can contribute more to them.", "D) Why some people are more passionate about work."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What have businesses come to recognize now?问当下企业认识到了什么，是问事实细\n节，不是推断。\n【拆句】What have businesses come to recognize now?现在完成时have come to recognize表\n示从过去到现在累积形成的认识，now强调当下状态，结构无陷阱。\n【定位】Most important, there is a growing recognition that people who balance work and play, and\nwho work at what they are passionate about, are more focused and productive, delivering\ngreater value to their clients.\n最重要的是，人们越来越认识到：那些工作与休息平衡、并从事自己所热爱之事的人，更专\n注、更高产，能给客户带来更大的价值。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴+偷换程度\nA项Who is capable of solving problems with ease.里solving problems with ease\n（轻松解决问题）来自solve problems their full-time teams can't，但P3说的是\"解决\n全职团队搞不定的问题\"，并非\"轻松\"，且和\"现在认识到的\"语境不符。\nB) 答非所问（偷换疑问词）\nB项How people can be more focused and productive.问的是\"如何变得更专注高\n产\"，而原文给的是\"哪类人更专注高产\"，疑问词错位，且companies的认知对象是人，\n不是方法。\nC) ✓ 正确\npeople who balance work and play, and who work at what they are passionate about, are more\nfocused and productive, delivering greater value to their clients\n↔ What kind of people can contribute more to them.\n原文that从句的真正主语是people who...，整句重心是这类人能为客户贡献更多价值；\nC项的\"What kind of people can contribute more to them\"精准对位该重心。\nD) 答非所问+偷换重心\nD项Why some people are more passionate about work.问\"为什么有些人对工作更\n热情\"，但原文里passionate只是描述这类人的特征之一，并未解释\"原因\"，且重心是其\n价值，不是热情的成因。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author say about the gig economy?", "options": ["A) It may force companies to reform their business practice.", "B) It may soon replace the traditional economic model.", "C) It will drive technological progress on a global scale.", "D) It will bring about radical economic and social changes."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about the gig economy?问的是作者对零工经济的具体陈述；\n答案直接在原文有对应句而非需要推断。\n【拆句】What does the author say about the gig economy?结构简单：主干What does the\nauthor say，about the gig economy是介词短语修饰say，无陷阱。\n【定位】Perhaps more important, as the global economy continues to be disrupted by technology\nand other massive change, the gig economy will itself become an engine of economic and\nsocial transformation.\n或许更重要的是，随着全球经济持续被技术和其他剧变所颠覆，零工经济本身将成为经济与\n社会变革的引擎。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nA项It may force companies to reform their business practice.说零工经济会\"迫\n使企业改革其业务实践\"，原文并未提到\"迫使企业改革\"这一动作，create a bridge\nbetween traditional enterprises and this emerging economy说的是搭桥而非迫使\n改革。\nB) 与原文相反\nB项It may soon replace the traditional economic model.说零工经济会\"很快取代传\n统经济模式\"，原文里始终是\"搭桥\"\"成为引擎\"，从未表达替代关系。\nC) 与原文相反（因果颠倒）\nC项It will drive technological progress on a global scale.说零工经济\"将驱动全球技\n术进步\"，但原文里是\"global economy被technology颠覆\"，技术是动因、零工经济是\n结果之一，方向被反过来。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthe gig economy will itself become an engine of economic and social transformation ↔\nIt will bring about radical economic and social changes.\n从预判\"将带来经济与社会变革\"出发，D项把engine of transformation改写成bring\nabout radical changes，逐字段语义匹配，是定位句的直接同义替换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
